hadiths[429]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3200","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded the beautification of the festivals through the remembrance of Allah's greatness. > **""Beautify your ʿĪds with Takbīr.""**"
"3201","Shurayḥ ibn Abrahah reports having seen the Messenger of Allah ﷺ uttering the Takbīr during the Days of Tashrīq, beginning from Ẓuhr prayer on the Day of Sacrifice and continuing after every prescribed prayer until his departure from Minā, a practice which the people of Madīnah likewise observed."
"3202","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه is reported to have maintained the Takbīr beginning from the Fajr prayer on the Day of ʿArafah until ʿAṣr prayer on the Day of Sacrifice."
"3203","ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit رضي الله عنه narrates from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning the reward of worship on the nights preceding the two festivals. > **""Whoever keeps alive the night of Fiṭr and the night of Aḍḥā, his heart will not die on the day when hearts die.""**"
"3204","Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh narrates from his father, from his grandfather, that the Prophet ﷺ used to perform ritual bathing for the two ʿĪds."
"3205","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the forgiveness granted to one who fasts, bathes, attends the prayer ground, and seals the deed with charity. > **""Whoever fasts Ramaḍān, and proceeds in the morning with ghusl to the muṣallā, and seals it with charity, returns forgiven.""**"
"3206","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports the practice of his people on ʿĪd al-Fiṭr of taking food and drink and performing ghusl before setting out for the prayer ground."
"3207","Hushaym asked Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād whether bathing is prescribed on days other than Friday, and he replied affirming it for the day of ʿArafah, which is a day of ʿĪd, the day of Fiṭr, the day of Aḍḥā, the day of ʿArafah, and the day of Friday."
"3208","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to wear a red outer cloak on the day of ʿĪd."
"3209","ʿAṭāʾ reports that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما enjoining that none should go out on the day of Fiṭr without first eating, a counsel ʿAṭāʾ thereafter always observed even with a morsel of thin bread and a drink of milk or water, understanding from the Prophet ﷺ that the people would delay going out until mid-morning lest haste disturb their prayer. > **""If you are able that none of you goes out on the day of Fiṭr until he eats, then let him do so.""**"
"3210","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would eat on the day of Fiṭr before going out in the morning, and would command the people to do the same. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would eat on the day of Fiṭr before going out in the morning, and he would command the people to do that.""**"
"3211","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما states that it is from the Sunnah to eat something before going out for ʿĪd, and in another wording not to go out on the day of Fiṭr until giving Ṣadaqah and eating something beforehand. > **""It is from the Sunnah that you eat before you go out, even if only a date.""**"
"3212","Jābir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would eat seven dates before going out on the day of Fiṭr, while on the day of Aḍḥā he would taste nothing beforehand."
"3213","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would eat on the day of Fiṭr before going out to the muṣallā."
"3214","Buraydah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not go out on the day of Fiṭr until he had eaten, whereas on the Day of Sacrifice he would eat nothing until his return, when he would eat from his sacrificial animal."
"3215","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would go out to ʿĪd accompanied by a spear and a shield."
"3216","Saʿd ibn ʿAmmār al-Qaraẓ, the muʾadhdhin of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, narrates that when the Prophet ﷺ delivered the khuṭbah on the two ʿĪds he would lean upon a bow."
"3217","Jābir رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would go out on ʿĪd and would bring his family with him."
"3218","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrates that in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, young maidens would come out from behind their curtains for the two ʿĪds."
"3219","The sister of ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah narrates from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that going out was made binding upon every grown maiden, meaning for the two ʿĪds. > **""Going out is obligatory upon every woman of mature age.""**"
"3220","Umm al-Muʾminīn ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrates that she asked the Prophet ﷺ whether women should go out on ʿĪd, and he affirmed it even for maidens, directing one without a garment to wear her companion's garment. > **""Yes. Yes. If she has no garment to wear, let her wear the garment of her companion.""**"
"3221","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ restricted the going out of women except under necessity, such as having no servant, apart from the two ʿĪds of Aḍḥā and Fiṭr, and restricted their share of the road to its edges. > **""Women have no share in going out except under compulsion, meaning one who has no servant, except for the two ʿĪds, al-Aḍḥā and al-Fiṭr; and they have no share of the road except its sides.""**"
"3222","ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAmr narrates from his father, from his grandfather, that on a day of ʿĪd, while in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ sent for the chief of the Anṣār, Ubayy ibn Kaʿb رضي الله عنه, instructing him to proceed to the muṣallā, order its sweeping, and command the people to come out; and when asked about women, he included maidens and menstruating women among those present for the supplication. > **""Call for me the chief of the Anṣār. O Ubayy, go to the muṣallā, order it to be swept, and order the people to come out.""** > **""The maidens and the menstruating women shall be among the people, witnessing the supplication.""**"
"3223","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would go out to ʿĪd on foot and would return by a route different from that by which he had gone."
"3224","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥāṭib reports having seen the Prophet ﷺ come to ʿĪd by one road and return by another."
"3225","Saʿīd ibn Aws al-Anṣārī narrates from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the angels standing at the entrances of the roads on the day of ʿĪd al-Fiṭr, calling the Muslims to a Generous Lord who bestows good and rewards it abundantly, reminding them of their night prayer, fasting, and obedience, and announcing forgiveness after the prayer, for which reason it is called the Day of Reward in the heavens. > **""When it is the day of ʿĪd al-Fiṭr, the angels stand at the entrances of the roads and call out: Proceed, O company of Muslims, to a Generous Lord who bestows good and then rewards it abundantly. You were commanded to stand at night and you stood; you were commanded to fast the day and you fasted, and you obeyed your Lord, so receive your rewards. Then when they have prayed, a caller announces: Indeed your Lord has forgiven you, so return rightly guided to your dwellings. It is the Day of Reward, and that day is named in heaven the Day of Reward. I have forgiven all your sins.""**"
"3226","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrates that the supplication of the Prophet ﷺ on the two ʿĪds asked for a guarded life, an upright death, a return without disgrace, protection from sudden ruin, chastity, sufficiency, piety, guidance, a good end in this world and the Hereafter, and refuge from doubt, discord, and ostentation, invoking the Turner of hearts for steadfastness and mercy. > **""O Allah, we ask You for a guarded life, an upright death, and a return that is neither disgraceful nor shameful. O Allah, do not destroy us suddenly, nor seize us unawares, nor hasten us before dues and bequests. O Allah, we ask You for chastity, sufficiency, piety, guidance, and a good end in the Hereafter and this world; and we seek refuge in You from doubt, discord, ostentation, and repute in Your religion. O Turner of hearts, do not cause our hearts to deviate after You have guided us, and grant us mercy from Yourself. Indeed You are the Bestower.""**"
"3227","Wahb ibn Kaysān reports hearing ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما on the day of ʿĪd, after praying before the khuṭbah and then standing to address the people, declare that all this is the established way of Allah and His Messenger. > **""O people, all of it is the Sunnah of Allah and the Sunnah of His Messenger.""**"
"3228","Anas رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar would begin with the prayer before the khuṭbah on ʿĪd, as on the Day of Sacrifice the Prophet ﷺ prayed and then addressed the people."
"3229","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would begin with the prayer on Fiṭr and Aḍḥā."
"3230","Ayyūb reports seeing Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه and al-Ḥasan praying on the day of ʿĪd before the imām came out, while Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn came and sat without praying; Anas is elsewhere reported to have prayed four rakʿahs."
"3231","Ibn Sīrīn and Qatādah report that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would pray four or eight rakʿahs after the Eid prayer, and would not pray before it."
"3232","Abū Masʿūd رضي الله عنه states that praying before the emergence of the imām on the day of ʿĪd is not from the Sunnah. > **""It is not from the Sunnah to pray before the emergence of the imām on the day of ʿĪd.""**"
"3233","Fāʾid Abū al-Warqāʾ reports leading ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā رضي الله عنه to the open prayer ground on a day of ʿĪd, seating him near the minbar where he sat without praying before or after, and informing that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not pray before it or after it."
"3234","Ibn Sīrīn reports that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه and Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه would forbid the people, or would seat whomever they saw praying before the emergence of the imām on ʿĪd."
"3235","ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah reports going with Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah رضي الله عنه to the muṣallā on ʿĪd, where he sat before the imām arrived and did not pray; and after the imām and the people departed toward the mosque, he described the contrary practice as innovation and abandonment of the Sunnah, saying the two rakʿahs of ʿĪd suffice as the supererogatory prayer of the day. > **""This is innovation and abandonment of the Sunnah. Much of what we see is harshness and lack of knowledge. These two rakʿahs are the supererogatory prayer of this day, until this prayer itself calls you.""**"
"3236","Al-Walīd ibn Sarīʿ, freedman of ʿAmr ibn Ḥurayth, reports going out with Amīr al-Muʾminīn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه on a day of ʿĪd, when questioners twice asked about prayer before and after the Eid prayer without receiving a reply; after ʿAlī led the people with seven and five Takbīrs, delivered the khuṭbah, and dismounted, and was asked about people praying, he explained the Sunnah that the Prophet ﷺ did not pray before or after it, leaving the matter to choice, and refused to prevent people from praying. > **""You asked me about the Sunnah. The Prophet ﷺ did not pray before it or after it. Whoever wishes may do so, and whoever wishes may leave it. Do you consider that I would prevent people who pray, and thus be like one who prevents a servant when he prays?""**"
"3237","Abū Rāfiʿ رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would go out to the two ʿĪds on foot and would pray without adhān or iqāmah."
"3238","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed on the day of Aḍḥā without adhān or iqāmah, addressed the men, then inclined toward the women, addressed them, and urged them to give charity until a great quantity of goods gathered with Bilāl رضي الله عنه."
"3239","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه narrates that the Prophet ﷺ prayed ʿĪd without adhān or iqāmah and delivered two khuṭbahs, separating them with a sitting."
"3240","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed ʿĪd as two rakʿahs in which he recited nothing but the Mother of the Book, adding nothing beyond it."
"3241","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite in the two ʿĪds ""Glorify the Name of your Lord, the Most High"" and ""Has there reached you the story of the Overwhelming."""
"3242","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would recite in the prayer of the two ʿĪds ""ʿAmma yatasāʾalūn"" and ""By the sun and its brightness."""
"3243","Al-Ḥārith narrates from ʿAlī رضي الله عنه that audible recitation in the Eid prayer is from the Sunnah. > **""Audible recitation in the Eid prayer is from the Sunnah.""**"
"3244","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه narrates that a small spear would be brought out for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the two ʿĪds toward which he would pray, that he would utter thirteen Takbīrs, and that Abū Bakr and ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما would do the same."
"3245","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that the Prophet ﷺ would utter twelve Takbīrs in the two ʿĪds, seven in the first rakʿah and five in the last, and that he would go by one road and return by another."
"3246","Abū Wāqid al-Laythī رضي الله عنه and ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها narrate that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led the people on Fiṭr and Aḍḥā, uttering seven Takbīrs in the first rakʿah and reciting ""Qāf, by the Glorious Qurʾān,"" and five in the second with ""The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split."""
"3247","Kurdūs reports that al-Walīd sent after ʿIshāʾ to ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Ḥudhayfah, Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, and Abū Masʿūd, asking how the prayer should be performed now that the Muslims' ʿĪd had come; they referred him to Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who instructed standing with four Takbīrs, reciting the Fātiḥah and a sūrah from the Muṣḥaṣṣal, then four Takbīrs bowing with the last of them, making nine in the two ʿĪds, which none of them rejected. > **""He stands and utters four Takbīrs, then recites the Fātiḥah of the Book and a sūrah from the Mufaṣṣal, then utters four Takbīrs, bowing with the last of them; thus nine in the two ʿĪds.""**"
"3248","Ibrāhīm reports that when al-Walīd ibn ʿUqbah entered the mosque, with Ibn Masʿūd, Ḥudhayfah, and Abū Mūsā in its courtyard, and asked what to do as ʿĪd had arrived, Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه instructed repeated Takbīr interspersed with praise of Allah, extolling Him, sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and supplicating, then Takbīr with recitation of the Fātiḥah and a sūrah, bowing and prostrating, then a second rakʿah likewise, a method which Ḥudhayfah and Abū Mūsā confirmed as correct. > **""Say: Allāhu Akbar, and praise Allah, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and supplicate Allah. Then utter Takbīr, praise Him, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and supplicate. Then utter Takbīr, praise Him, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and supplicate. Then utter Takbīr, praise Him, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, and supplicate. Then utter Takbīr and recite the Fātiḥah of the Book and a sūrah. Then utter Takbīr, bow, and prostrate. Then stand and recite the Fātiḥah and a sūrah. Then utter Takbīr, praise Allah, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, supplicate, then utter Takbīr, praise Allah, extol Him, send blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then bow and prostrate.""**"
"3249","Kurdūs reports that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would utter nine Takbīrs in Aḍḥā and Fiṭr, beginning with four, then reciting, then one Takbīr with which to bow, then in the second rakʿah reciting first and uttering four Takbīrs, bowing with one of them."
"3250","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه states that between every two Takbīrs there should be pause enough for a phrase. > **""Between every two Takbīrs is the measure of a phrase.""**"
"3251","ʿAbdullāh states that the Takbīr in the two ʿĪds is four, like the prayer over the deceased. > **""The Takbīr in the two ʿĪds is four, like the prayer over the deceased.""**"
"3252","Abū Ṭarafah ʿAbbād ibn al-Rayyān al-Lakhmī al-Ḥimṣī reports visiting al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib رضي الله عنه in a village some miles from Ḥimṣ on a day of ʿĪd and asking him to come out and lead them in ʿĪd, but he refused and told them to pray individually. > **""No. Pray individually.""**"
"3253","Al-Shaʿbī reports that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that whoever misses ʿĪd should pray four rakʿahs. > **""Whoever misses ʿĪd, let him pray four.""**"
"3254","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ delivered the khuṭbah on the day of ʿĪd while upon his mount."
"3255","Ḥabīb ibn ʿUmar al-Anṣārī narrates from his father that he met Wāthilah on a day of ʿĪd and said, ""May Allah accept from us and from you,"" to which Wāthilah replied in like manner. > **""Yes, may Allah accept from us and from you.""**"
"3256","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه states that going out to the open prayer ground on the two ʿĪds is from the Sunnah. > **""Going out to the open ground on the two ʿĪds is from the Sunnah.""**"
"3257","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه states in another narration that praying in the open ground is from the Sunnah. > **""Praying in the open ground is from the Sunnah.""**"
"3258","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUthmān al-Taymī رضي الله عنه reports seeing the Messenger of Allah ﷺ standing in the market on the day of ʿĪd watching the people pass, and in another wording standing in the middle of the muṣallā after departing from the two ʿĪds, observing how the people dispersed and what their demeanour was, pausing a while before departing himself."
"3259","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrates that a slave-girl of Ḥassān ibn Thābit came to them on a day of Fiṭr with hair uncovered, carrying a tambourine and singing, and when Umm Salamah rebuked her, the Prophet ﷺ told her to leave her, for every people has an ʿĪd and this is ours. > **""Leave her, O Umm Salamah, for every people has an ʿĪd, and this is our ʿĪd.""**"
"3260","Zaynab bint Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها narrates that entertainers would play while the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was in the mosque."
"3261","Abū Shurayḥ al-Khuzāʿī رضي الله عنه narrates that when the sun eclipsed in the caliphate of ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه, with ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd in Madīnah, ʿUthmān went out and led the people in two rakʿahs with two prostrations in each; thereafter Ibn Masʿūd, seated by the chamber of ʿĀʾishah, related that the Prophet ﷺ would command prayer at solar and lunar eclipses, urging haste to prayer whether the feared event befalls or good is thereby gained. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would command prayer at the eclipse of the sun and the moon. When you see it afflicting them, hasten to prayer; for if it be what you fear, you will not be heedless, and if it be not, you will have attained and earned good.""**"
"3262","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه is reported to have led the people in eclipse prayer, reciting Yā-Sīn and the like, bowing for about the length of the sūrah, rising with ""Allah hears him who praises Him,"" standing in supplication and Takbīr for a like span, and repeating until four bowings, then prostrating, performing the second rakʿah likewise, continuing in supplication until the sun cleared, and informing them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had done the same."
"3263","Maḥmūd ibn Labīd رضي الله عنه narrates that when the sun eclipsed on the day Ibrāhīm, the son of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, died, and the people attributed it to his death, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the sun and moon to be two signs among Allah's signs, unaffected by any death or life, commanding recourse to the mosques; he then stood, recited part of al-Dhāriyāt, bowed, straightened, prostrated twice, and repeated the same in the next standing. > **""The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. They do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life. When you see them thus, hasten to the mosques.""**"
"3264","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that he prayed the eclipse prayer behind the Prophet ﷺ and did not hear a single letter from him therein."
"3265","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه is reported to have stood at an eclipse of the sun, bowing five times with two prostrations, doing the same in the second rakʿah, and declaring that none after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed it thus besides himself. > **""None after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ has prayed it but I.""**"
"3266","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه narrates that when the sun eclipsed on the day Ibrāhīm died, the Prophet ﷺ said that the sun and moon are two signs, continuing with words like the narration at the beginning of the chapter. > **""The sun and the moon are two signs.""**"
"3267","Bilāl رضي الله عنه narrates that when the sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he declared that the sun and moon do not eclipse for anyone's death or life but are two signs among Allah's signs, commanding prayer like the most recent prayer they had prayed when they saw it. > **""The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah. When you see that, pray like the most recent prayer you have prayed.""**"
"3268","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما narrates that when the sun eclipsed upon the death of a great man, the Prophet ﷺ went out and led the people with prolonged standing until it was said he would not bow, prolonged bowing until it was said he would not rise, then a second standing and bowing like the first, then prostration, repeating the same in the second rakʿah with four bowings and four prostrations, then turning to the people and declaring the sun and moon to be signs unaffected by death or life, commanding recourse to prayer. > **""O people, the sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah. When you see them, hasten to prayer.""**"
"3269","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed at the eclipse of the sun, standing with Takbīr and recitation, bowing for the length of his recitation, rising for the length of his bowing, and repeating this four times before the two prostrations, doing the same in the second rakʿah without reciting between the bowings."
"3270","Samurah رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say that the sun and moon do not eclipse for anyone's death but are two signs by which Allah admonishes His servants, to see who fears and remembers Him, commanding recourse to His remembrance. > **""The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of any one of you, but they are two signs among the signs of Allah by which He admonishes His servants, to see who fears Him and who remembers Him. When you see that, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and remember Him.""**"
"3271","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما narrates that when the sun eclipsed in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and the people said it was sorcery upon the sun, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited: ""The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split. And if they see a sign, they turn away and say: Continuing sorcery."" > **""The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split. And if they see a sign, they turn away and say: Continuing sorcery.""**"
"3272","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه narrates that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say that the sun and moon do not eclipse for anyone's death nor for any new event, but are among Allah's signs by which His servants take heed; and that after leading them in prayer on the day the sun darkened, he exhorted them, declaring that whatever of this world, Paradise, or Hell they had seen or been told of had been imaged before him toward the wall since he began that prayer, which he beheld depicted in the wall of the mosque. > **""The sun and the moon do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for anything you innovate, but that is among the signs of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, by which His servants take heed. He rewards whoever fears Him and remembers Him. When you see some of the signs of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, hasten to the remembrance of Allah, remember Him, and fear Him.""** > **""Nothing you have seen in this world possessing colour, nor anything you have been told of in Paradise or in Hell, but it has been imaged for me toward this wall since I prayed this prayer for you, and I looked upon it imaged in the wall of the mosque.""**"
"3273","Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbbād al-ʿAbdī of Baṣrah reports witnessing a khuṭbah of Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه in which he related that while he and an Anṣārī youth were shooting arrows in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the sun, at the height of two or three spears to the beholder's eye, darkened until it shone as though veiled; one said to the other that they should go to the mosque, for this affair of the sun would surely bring forth something new for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning his Ummah; arriving to find him come forth to the people, he led them in the longest prayer they had ever stood, without them hearing his voice, then the longest prostration, repeating the same in the second rakʿah until the sun cleared as he sat; he then greeted, praised and extolled Allah, testified that he is His servant and Messenger, and adjured the people whether he had fallen short in conveying his Lord's messages, to which men stood testifying that he had conveyed, sincerely advised, and fulfilled his charge; he then declared that men allege this eclipse of sun and moon and displacement of stars from their risings to be due to deaths. > **""O people, I adjure you by Allah: if you know that I have fallen short in anything of conveying the messages of my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, then inform me of it. I have conveyed the messages of my Lord as they ought to be conveyed; and if you know that I have conveyed the messages of my Lord, then inform me of it.""** > **""As for after: men allege that the eclipse of this sun and the eclipse of this moon and the removal of these stars from their risings are on account of the death of so-and-so.""**"
"3274","ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه narrates that when Ibrāhīm died and the sun eclipsed, the people said it had eclipsed for his death, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared the sun and moon to be two signs among Allah's signs, eclipsing for neither death nor life, commanding recourse to prayer upon seeing it. > **""The sun and the moon are two signs among the signs of Allah. They do not eclipse for the death of anyone nor for his life. When you see that, hasten to prayer.""**"
"3275","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the moon eclipsed during the lifetime of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, after which the narrator related a report similar to the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj concerning the eclipse."
"3276","Mūsā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān reported from Umm Sufyān that a Jewish woman used to visit ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها and converse with her, and upon leaving would invoke protection for her from the punishment of the grave. When the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ came, ʿĀʾishah informed him of this, and he responded concerning her statement. Then the sun eclipsed, so he sought refuge in Allāh from the punishment of the grave, pronounced Takbīr, and stood for a prolonged standing, then bowed for a prolonged bowing, then raised his head and stood again at length, then bowed again at length though shorter than the first, and he prayed two rakʿahs with two bowings and two prostrations, his standings and bowings therein being of similar length. > **""She has lied; that is only for the People of the Book. I seek refuge in Allāh from the punishment of the grave.""**"
"3277","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that when there was a night of violent wind, the refuge of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was the mosque until the wind subsided, and when any sign appeared in the sky such as an eclipse of the sun or the moon, his refuge was prayer until it cleared."
"3278","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that their Lord Almighty declared that were His servants to obey Him, He would grant them rain by night, cause the sun to rise upon them by day, and would not let them hear the sound of thunder. In an addition, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded renewal of faith, and when asked how to renew it, he directed them to the statement of lā ilāha illā Allāh. > **""Your Lord Almighty has said: Had My servants obeyed Me, I would have given them rain by night, and caused the sun to rise upon them by day, and would not have made them hear the sound of thunder. Renew your faith. Renew your faith by saying: Lā ilāha illā Allāh.""**"
"3279","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that when the wind raged violently, its effect would be recognised upon the face of the Prophet ﷺ."
"3280","Muʿāwiyah al-Laythī رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the people would suffer drought, then Allāh Blessed and Exalted would send down upon them provision from His provision, yet they would become polytheists in the morning. When asked how that would be, he explained it by their claim of having been rained upon by such and such a star. > **""The people will suffer drought, then Allāh Blessed and Exalted will send down upon them provision from His provision, and they will become polytheists in the morning. They will say: We were rained upon by such and such a star.""**"
"3281","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that rain failed during the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so they asked the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ to pray for rain for them, and he did so. The next morning the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ came upon a group conversing and claiming they had been rained upon during the night by such and such a star, whereupon the Prophet of Allāh ﷺ remarked upon how no blessing is bestowed upon a people except that they become ungrateful for it by morning. > **""Allāh does not bestow any blessing upon a people except that they become disbelievers therein by morning.""**"
"3282","Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAwf reported that he asked Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما about the Sunnah concerning the Istisqāʾ prayer, and he replied that its Sunnah is like the Sunnah of the ʿĪd prayer. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had gone out to pray for rain and prayed two rakʿahs in which he recited, pronouncing seven Takbīrs in the first and five Takbīrs in the second."
"3283","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reported that the people suffered barrenness during the time of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, so the Muslims came to him complaining of withheld rain, dried trees, perishing livestock, and afflicted people, asking him to pray to his Lord for rain. He appointed a day and instructed them to come out with their charities. On that day the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and the people walked out with tranquillity and dignity until they reached the muṣallā, where the Prophet ﷺ advanced and led them in two rakʿahs with audible recitation. He would recite in the two ʿĪds and in Istisqāʾ al-Fātiḥah and Sabbiḥ isma rabbika al-Aʿlā in the first rakʿah, and al-Fātiḥah and Hal atāka ḥadīth al-Ghāshiyah in the second. When he finished the prayer he faced the people, turned his cloak inside out, knelt upon his knees, raised his hands, pronounced a Takbīr before supplicating, and then supplicated at length for abundant, beneficial, and immediate rain. The narrator said they had not yet dispersed when clouds gathered together and it rained upon them for seven days and nights continuously without ceasing over Madīnah. > **""O Allāh, grant us a rescuing, spacious, spring-like, fertile, copious, layered, drenching, wholesome, verdant, fruitful, pouring, encompassing, trailing, swift, lasting, abundant, beneficial, harmless rain, immediate and not delayed. O Allāh, give life thereby to the lands, rescue thereby the servants, make it provision for those present among us and those in the desert. O Allāh, send down upon our land its adornment and its tranquillity. O Allāh, send down upon us pure water from the sky, give life thereby to a dead town, and quench therewith what You created of cattle and many people.""**"
"3284","Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنهما and Anas رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed for rain he would supplicate for a gentle, beneficial watering that would satiate wealth and souls, a wholesome, pleasant, layered, and encompassing rain by which desert and town would be eased, through which the blessings of heaven would descend and the blessings of earth would emerge, and that they be made grateful thereat, for He is the Hearer of supplication. > **""O Allāh, grant us a gentle, beneficial watering by which You satiate wealth and souls, a wholesome, pleasant, layered, encompassing rain by which our desert and our town are eased, through which You send down the blessings of the heaven and bring forth for us the blessings of the earth, and make us grateful thereat. Indeed You are the Hearer of supplication.""**"
"3285","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed for rain, supplicating for a rescuing, verdant, layered, immediate, undelayed, beneficial, and harmless rain. Shortly thereafter they were rained upon until everything flowed, and they came to him complaining of drowning, so the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ supplicated for the rain to be around them and not upon them. > **""O Allāh, grant us a rescuing, verdant, layered, immediate, undelayed, beneficial, harmless rain. O Allāh, around us and not upon us.""**"
"3286","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stood in the mosque in the forenoon, pronounced three Takbīrs, and then supplicated thrice for water and for provision of ghee, milk, fat, and meat, though no cloud was visible in the sky. Then a wind with dust arose, clouds gathered, the sky poured forth, the people of the markets cried out and rushed to the roofed parts of the mosque and to their homes while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ remained standing. The road flowed, and they saw the rain upon the edges of the hair of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and upon his shoulders like pearls. The Prophet ﷺ departed remarking upon its freshness from its Lord. Abū Umāmah said he never saw a year of greater abundance in ghee, milk, fat, and meat, so plentiful along the road that scarcely anyone would buy. Then he turned to the men and admonished and forbade them, then to the women, admonishing them and stressing the matter of silk and gold. A man of Banū ʿĀmir objected that he loved beauty, even to the extent of making the sheath of his whip from leopard skin, whereupon the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ clarified that Allāh is Beautiful and loves beauty, while pride is ignorance of truth and contempt of people. > **""O Allāh, grant us water. O Allāh, grant us water. O Allāh, grant us water. O Allāh, provide us with ghee, milk, fat, and meat. This is your most recent from its Lord. Indeed Allāh is Beautiful and loves beauty; pride is only he who is ignorant of the truth and despises people with his eyes.""**"
"3287","ʿUmar ibn Khārijah ibn Saʿd reported from his grandfather Saʿd رضي الله عنه that a group complained to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ of withheld rain, so he commanded them to kneel upon their knees, and they did so, then instructed them to say Yā Rabb. They did so and were given rain until they wished it to be removed from them. In another wording he commanded them to kneel and say Yā Rabb, Yā Rabb, raising his forefinger to the heaven, and they were rained upon until they wished it to cease. > **""Kneel upon your knees and say: Yā Rabb, Yā Rabb.""**"
"3288","Raqīqah bint Abī Ṣayfī ibn Hāshim, the mother of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib, reported that successive barren years befell Quraysh, drying udders and emaciating bones. While she lay distressed, a caller with a hoarse voice proclaimed that the Prophet was sent, his days overshadowing them and the time of his stars, urging them toward water and fertility by bringing forth a distinguished, noble, corpulent, fair, radiant man of Quraysh with thick eyelashes, smooth cheeks, and an aquiline nose, restraining anger and guided by tradition, together with his children and a man from every clan, after washing, perfuming, touching the Corner, ascending Abū Qubays, with the man supplicating and the people saying āmīn, so that they would be rained upon as they wished. In the morning she recounted her vision throughout the ravines of Makkah, and all declared it meant Shaybah al-Ḥamd, to whom the affairs of Quraysh were brought. Men from every clan came to him, washed, walked, touched the Corner, ascended Abū Qubays, and gathered around him until they stood at the peak. ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib stood with him the young boy, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, near maturity, raised his hands and supplicated to Allāh, the Filler of need and Remover of distress, the Teacher who is not taught and the Asked who is not miserly, complaining of their barren year that had removed hoof and cloven hoof, asking for drenching, verdant rain. By the Lord of the Kaʿbah, they had scarcely moved before the sky burst with water and the valley filled with its torrent, and she heard the elders of Quraysh, including ʿAbd Allāh ibn Judʿān and others, praising ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib for one granted water through his face. > **""O Allāh, Filler of need, Remover of distress, You teach and are not taught, You are asked and are not miserly. These are Your male and female servants at the sanctuaries of Your Sanctuary complaining to You of their barren year which has removed hoof and cloven hoof. O Allāh, so rain upon us a drenching, verdant rain.""**"
"3289","Abū Lubābah ibn ʿAbd al-Mundhir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed for rain, whereupon Abū Lubābah remarked that the dates were in the storehouses. The Prophet ﷺ then supplicated that they be rained upon until Abū Lubābah would stand bare and block the outlet of his storehouse with his waist-wrapper, though no cloud was visible in the sky. It rained so continuously that the people gathered to Abū Lubābah and told him it would not cease until he stood bare and blocked the outlet of his storehouse with his waist-wrapper. He did so, and the sky cleared. > **""O Allāh, grant us rain until Abū Lubābah stands bare and blocks the outlet of his storehouse with his waist-wrapper.""**"
"3290","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw rain, he would supplicate for it to be a beneficial downpour. > **""O Allāh, a beneficial downpour.""**"
"3291","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reported that when the Prophet ﷺ prayed for rain he would supplicate for its blessing, adornment, and tranquillity to be sent down in their land. In one narration there was the addition of asking for provision, for He is the Best of providers. > **""O Allāh, send down in our land its blessing, its adornment, and its tranquillity. Provide us, and You are the Best of providers.""**"
"3292","Al-Shifāʾ Umm Sulaymān رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ prayed for rain on Friday in the mosque, raising his hands and commanding seeking forgiveness of their Lord, for He is Most Forgiving, and he turned his cloak inside out. > **""Seek forgiveness of your Lord; indeed He is Most Forgiving.""**"
"3293","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would raise his hands when delivering the khuṭbah until the whiteness of his armpits could be seen."
"3294","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Khaṭmī reported that Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما went out with the people to pray for rain, delivered the khuṭbah, then prayed without adhān or iqāmah, while among the people on that day were al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه and Zayd ibn Arqam رضي الله عنه."
"3295","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that when rain befell them in Madīnah and the spouts flowed, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ declared that there would be no hardship upon them that year. > **""There is no hardship upon you this year.""**"
"3296","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said that the downpour is from there, pointing with his hand toward the heaven. > **""Indeed the downpour is here.""**"
"3297","Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf reported that he sat beside Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān when a handsome elderly man of Banū Ghifār with impaired hearing passed by. Ḥumayd sent for him, made space for him between them as a Companion of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and asked him to narrate a ḥadīth from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. The Shaykh stated that he heard the Messenger of Allāh say that Allāh Almighty originates the clouds, which then speak with the finest speech and laugh with the finest laughter. > **""Indeed Allāh Almighty originates the clouds, and they speak with the finest speech and laugh with the finest laughter.""**"
"3298","Sabrah ibn Maʿbad reported that the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ saw a cloud and expressed hope that it would rain upon them, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ informed them that it had been commanded to rain at Balīl, meaning a valley called Balīl. > **""Indeed it has been commanded to rain at Balīl.""**"
"3299","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ said that the south wind never stirs the depth of a valley's hollow without causing it to flow. > **""The south wind never stirs a hollow of a valley's hollows without causing it to flow.""**"
"3300","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when a sea-born cloud rises and then turns toward Syria, it is a copious spring of rain. > **""When a sea-born cloud rises and then turns toward Syria, it is a copious spring.""**"
"3301","Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that his close friend ﷺ counselled him with three matters: fasting three days of every month, performing Witr before sleeping, and the two rakʿahs of Fajr."
"3302","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that a man asked for direction to a deed by which Allāh would benefit him, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to hold to the two rakʿahs of Fajr, for therein lies virtue. > **""Hold to the two rakʿahs of Fajr, for therein lies virtue.""**"
"3303","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbid abandoning the two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer, for therein lie precious rewards. He also heard him forbid disowning one's child lest Allāh disgrace him before all creation as he disgraced the child in this world. He further heard him forbid dying with debt outstanding, for there will only be good deeds and evil deeds, with neither dīnār nor dirham for recompense and settlement, and Allāh wrongs no one. > **""Do not abandon the two rakʿahs before the Fajr prayer, for therein lie precious rewards. Do not disown your child, lest Allāh disgrace you before all creation as you disgraced him in this world. Do not die with debt upon you, for there will only be good deeds and evil deeds; there is neither dīnār nor dirham there for recompense and settlement, and Allāh wrongs no one.""**"
"3304","A man of the people of Ṣanʿāʾ reported that while in Makkah they sat beside ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī by the wall of the mosque without questioning him or his narrating to them. Then they sat with Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما in a similar gathering without questioning or narration, until he asked why they did not speak or remember Allāh, urging them to say Allāhu Akbar, al-Ḥamdu lillāh, and Subḥān Allāh wa bi-ḥamdih, whereby one counts as ten and ten as a hundred, with increase for whoever increases, and forgiveness for whoever remains silent. He then asked whether he should inform them of five matters he heard from the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and upon their assent he mentioned guarding the two rakʿahs of Fajr for their precious rewards. > **""Say: Allāhu Akbar, al-Ḥamdu lillāh, and Subḥān Allāh wa bi-ḥamdih; one counts as ten and ten as a hundred. Whoever increases, Allāh increases for him, and whoever remains silent, Allāh forgives him. Guard the two rakʿahs of Fajr, for therein lie precious rewards.""**"
"3305","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad equals a third of the Qurʾān, and Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn equals a quarter of the Qurʾān. He would recite them in the two rakʿahs of Fajr, declaring that in those two rakʿahs lies the aspiration of all time. > **""Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad equals a third of the Qurʾān, and Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn equals a quarter of the Qurʾān. In these two rakʿahs lies the aspiration of all time.""**"
"3306","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would recite Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn and Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad in the two rakʿahs of Fajr."
"3307","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would pray two rakʿahs after Witr in which he recited Qul Yā Ayyuhā al-Kāfirūn and Qul Huwa Allāhu Aḥad."
"3308","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that there is no prayer before Fajr except the two rakʿahs of Fajr. > **""There is no prayer before Fajr except the two rakʿahs of Fajr.""**"
"3309","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that when Fajr rises, there is no prayer except the two rakʿahs of Fajr. > **""When Fajr rises, there is no prayer except the two rakʿahs of Fajr.""**"
"3310","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ had prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr, he would recline upon his right side."
"3311","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would pray the two rakʿahs before Fajr, then supplicate for refuge from the Fire through the Lord of Jibrīl, Mīkāʾīl, Isrāfīl, and Muḥammad, then go out to his prayer. > **""O Allāh, Lord of Jibrīl, Mīkāʾīl, Isrāfīl, and Muḥammad, I seek refuge in You from the Fire.""**"
"3312","Usāmah ibn ʿUmayr رضي الله عنه reported that he prayed the two rakʿahs of Fajr with the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, praying close to him. The Prophet ﷺ prayed two light rakʿahs, and he heard him thrice invoke the Lord of Jibrīl, Mīkāʾīl, Isrāfīl, and Muḥammad for refuge from the Fire. > **""Lord of Jibrīl, Mīkāʾīl, Isrāfīl, and Muḥammad, I seek refuge in You from the Fire.""**"
"3313","Abū ʿUbaydah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd reported that it was grievous to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه to speak after Fajr except with the remembrance of Allāh. In another narration, it grieved him to hear anyone speaking from the rising of Fajr until the performance of the morning prayer."
"3314","ʿAṭāʾ reported that Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه came upon a group conversing after Fajr and forbade them from talk, declaring that they had come only for prayer, so they should either pray or remain silent."
"3315","Thawbān رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ liked to pray some of the middle of the day, whereupon ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها observed his fondness for prayer at that hour. He explained that therein the gates of heaven are opened and Allāh Blessed and Exalted looks with mercy upon His creation, and that it is a prayer observed regularly by Ādam, Nūḥ, Ibrāhīm, Mūsā, and ʿĪsā. > **""Therein the gates of heaven are opened, and Allāh Blessed and Exalted looks with mercy upon His creation. It is a prayer regularly observed by Ādam, Nūḥ, Ibrāhīm, Mūsā, and ʿĪsā.""**"
"3316","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported that when the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stayed with him, he saw him persist in four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, explaining that when the sun declines the gates of heaven are opened and none is closed until Ẓuhr is prayed, and he loves that good from him be raised therein. > **""When the sun declines, the gates of heaven are opened, and none of them is closed until Ẓuhr is prayed, so I love that good from me be raised in that hour.""**"
"3317","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ stayed with him for a month, and he observed that when the sun inclined or declined, whether engaged in worldly work or asleep, he would leave it or rise as though awakened, then perform ghusl or wuḍūʾ, and pray four rakʿahs completing and perfecting their bowing with composure. When he sought to depart, Abū Ayyūb asked him about this practice, and the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ explained that the gates of heaven and the gates of Paradise are opened in that hour, none fulfilling this prayer therein, and he loved that good from him ascend to his Lord in that hour. > **""Indeed the gates of heaven and the gates of Paradise are opened in that hour, and no one fulfils this prayer therein, so I have loved that good from me ascend to my Lord in that hour.""**"
"3318","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reported that when midday came, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would go out to one of the gardens of Madīnah where purification water was prepared for him. If he needed to relieve himself he did so, otherwise he purified himself. When the sun declined from the middle of the heaven by about a sandal-strap's measure, he stood and prayed four rakʿahs without Tashahhud between them, saluting at the end of the four, then rose and came to the mosque. When Ibn ʿAbbās asked what prayer this was which they did not pray, he declared that whoever of his Ummah prays them has enlivened his night, for it is an hour in which the gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered. > **""Whoever of my Ummah prays them has enlivened his night; it is an hour in which the gates of heaven are opened and supplication is answered.""**"
"3319","Ṣafwān رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever prays four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, they will be for him like the reward of ten necks, or he said like four necks from the children of Ismāʿīl. > **""Whoever prays four before Ẓuhr, they will be for him like the reward of ten necks, or four necks from the children of Ismāʿīl.""**"
"3320","Ayman, the freed slave of Ibn Abī ʿAmrah, reported that he entered upon ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها while still enslaved before his emancipation, and asked her at which hour the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ most frequently prayed. She replied that it was from the decline of the sun until it inclined."
"3321","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that whenever he came at midday, he found the Prophet ﷺ praying."
"3322","Al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever prays four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, it is as one who prayed Tahajjud with them in his night, and whoever prays them after ʿIshāʾ, it is like the same in Laylat al-Qadr. > **""Whoever prays four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr, it is as one who prayed Tahajjud with them in his night, and whoever prays them after ʿIshāʾ, it is like the same in Laylat al-Qadr.""**"
"3323","Al-Barāʾ رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would pray four rakʿahs before Ẓuhr."
"3324","Bashīr ibn Abī Sulaymān reported from an elderly Anṣārī Shaykh, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, that whoever prays four before Ẓuhr, it is equivalent to a neck from the children of Ismāʿīl. > **""Whoever prays four before Ẓuhr, it is equivalent to a neck from the children of Ismāʿīl.""**"
"3325","Bashīr ibn Sulaymān reported from ʿAmr al-Anṣārī, from his father, from the Prophet ﷺ, the like thereof concerning four before Ẓuhr. > **""Whoever prays four before Ẓuhr, it is equivalent to a neck from the children of Ismāʿīl.""**"
"3326","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever prays Ḍuḥā and four before Ḍuḥā, a house will be built for him in Paradise. > **""Whoever prays Ḍuḥā and four before Ḍuḥā, a house will be built for him in Paradise.""**"
"3327","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd reported from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that the midday prayer is like the night prayer. When ʿAbd al-Raḥmān was asked about al-Hajīr, he explained it as when the sun has declined. > **""The midday prayer is like the night prayer.""**"
"3328","ʿAbd Allāh ibn Budayl reported from the closest companion of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه that when the sun declined, he would stand and pray four rakʿahs, reciting therein two sūrahs from the Miʾīn, and when the muʾadhdhins called to one another he would tighten his garments and go out to prayer."
"3329","Al-Aswad, Murrah, and Masrūq reported that ʿAbd Allāh رضي الله عنه said that nothing of daytime prayer equals night prayer except four before Ẓuhr, whose superiority over daytime prayer is like the superiority of congregational prayer over solitary prayer."
"3330","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would pray between Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr."
"3331","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ would pray two rakʿahs before ʿAṣr."
"3332","Umm Ḥabībah bint Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that whoever guards four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, Allāh Almighty will build for him a house in Paradise. > **""Whoever guards four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, Allāh Almighty will build for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"3333","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported from the Prophet ﷺ that whoever prays four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, Allāh will forbid his body to the Fire. When she remarked that she had seen him sometimes pray them and sometimes omit them, he replied that he is not like any one of them. > **""Whoever prays four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, Allāh will forbid his body to the Fire. I am not like any one of you.""**"
"3334","ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ رضي الله عنهما reported that he came while the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ sat among some of his Companions including ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه, catching the end of the discourse as the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ declared that whoever prays four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, the Fire will not touch him, and in another wording that Allāh will forbid him to the Fire. > **""Whoever prays four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr, the Fire will not touch him. Allāh will forbid him to the Fire.""**"
"3335","ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said that his Ummah will continue praying these four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr until they walk upon the earth decisively forgiven. > **""My Ummah will continue praying these four rakʿahs before ʿAṣr until they walk upon the earth decisively forgiven.""**"
"3336","ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that ʿUmar رضي الله عنه went out to the people and struck them for the two prostrations after ʿAṣr, until he passed Tamīm al-Dārī رضي الله عنه, who refused to abandon them, declaring he had prayed them with one better than ʿUmar, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. ʿUmar replied that had the people been like him, he would not care."
"3337","ʿUrwah reported from Tamīm al-Dārī رضي الله عنه that after ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه forbade prayer after ʿAṣr, Tamīm bowed two rakʿahs, whereupon ʿUmar struck him with the whip. Tamīm gestured to him to sit while in prayer, and ʿUmar sat until Tamīm finished. When Tamīm asked why he was struck, ʿUmar cited his prohibition, and Tamīm replied that he had prayed them with one better than him, the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ. ʿUmar then explained he bore no sin toward the group, but feared that after him people would pray between ʿAṣr and Maghrib until entering the hour in which the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ definitively forbade prayer, while praying between Ẓuhr and ʿAṣr and claiming precedent of others praying after ʿAṣr."
"3338","Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī رضي الله عنه reported that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb رضي الله عنه, while caliph, saw him bow two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr, walked to him and struck him with the whip while he continued praying. When he finished, Zayd swore by Allāh that he would never abandon them after seeing the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ pray them. ʿUmar sat with him and explained that were it not for fear that people would take them as a ladder to prayer until nightfall, he would not strike concerning them."
"3339","Abū Mūsā رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ pray two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr. Abū Dirās added that he saw Abū Bakr ibn Abī Mūsā pray them, stating he saw Abū Mūsā pray them and declare that the Prophet ﷺ would pray them in the house of ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها."
"3340","ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal reported that Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān رضي الله عنه led them in ʿAṣr prayer, then sent a messenger to Maymūnah رضي الله عنها followed by another man. She informed them that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was equipping an expedition and lacked charity camels, so he sat distributing among them and was detained until ʿAṣr became pressing. He would pray two rakʿahs and whatever Allāh willed before ʿAṣr, then prayed ʿAṣr, returned, and prayed what he used to pray before it, for when he prayed a prayer or did a deed he loved to persist upon it."
"3341","Maymūnah رضي الله عنها reported that the two rakʿahs of ʿAṣr escaped the Prophet ﷺ, so he prayed them afterwards."
"3342","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reported that two rakʿahs before ʿAṣr escaped the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ, and when he finished he prayed them, then never prayed them again afterwards."
"3343","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed ʿAṣr, then entered her house and prayed two rakʿahs. When she asked about this prayer he had not previously prayed, he explained that wealth had come and distracted him from two rakʿahs he would bow after Ẓuhr, so he prayed them now. When she asked whether they should be made up when missed, he replied in the negative. > **""Wealth came to me and distracted me from two rakʿahs I would bow after Ẓuhr, so I have prayed them now. No.""**"
"3344","Qabīṣah ibn Dhuʾayb reported that ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها informed the family of al-Zubayr that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ prayed two rakʿahs after ʿAṣr with her, so they would pray them. Qabīṣah said Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه remarked that Allāh forgive ʿĀʾishah, for they knew the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ better than her. Bedouins had come to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ at midday, sitting and questioning him while he gave them rulings until he prayed Ẓuhr without praying after it, then sat giving rulings until he prayed ʿAṣr. He then returned to his house, remembered he had prayed nothing after Ẓuhr, and prayed them after ʿAṣr. Zayd insisted they knew the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ better than ʿĀʾishah, for the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbade prayer after ʿAṣr. > **""The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ forbade prayer after ʿAṣr.""**"
"3345","Zayd ibn Thābit رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ forbade praying when the edge of the sun rises or when its edge sets, for it rises between the two horns of Satan. > **""Do not pray when the edge of the sun rises or when its edge sets, for it rises between the two horns of Satan.""**"
"3346","Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Prophet ﷺ about the hour of night and day in which prayer is disliked, being ignorant of what he knew. The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ instructed him to refrain after Fajr until the sun rises, then pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until it stands over his head like a spear. When it stands directly overhead, that is an hour in which Hell is stoked and its gates opened, until it passes his right eyebrow. Once it has declined from his right eyebrow, he may pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until ʿAṣr is prayed. > **""When you have prayed Fajr, refrain from prayer until the sun rises; when it has risen, pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until it stands over your head like a spear. When it stands over your head, that hour Hell is stoked and its gates are opened, until it passes your right eyebrow. When it has passed your right eyebrow, pray, for prayer is attended and accepted until you pray ʿAṣr.""**"
"3347","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ say that there are two prayers after which no prayer is performed: Fajr until the sun rises, and ʿAṣr until the sun sets. > **""Two prayers after which there is no prayer: Fajr until the sun rises, and ʿAṣr until the sun sets.""**"
"3348","Murrah ibn Kaʿb or Kaʿb ibn Murrah al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ which part of the night is most heard, and he replied the last depth of the night. He then explained that prayer is accepted until dawn breaks, then there is no prayer until the sun rises to a spear or two, then prayer is accepted until the shadow stands like a spear, then there is no prayer until the sun declines, then prayer is accepted until ʿAṣr is prayed, then there is no prayer until the sun sets. > **""The last depth of the night. Prayer is accepted until dawn breaks, then there is no prayer until the sun rises to a spear or two. Then prayer is accepted until the shadow stands like a spear. Then there is no prayer until the sun declines. Then prayer is accepted until ʿAṣr is prayed. Then there is no prayer until the sun sets.""**"
"3349","Abū Umāmah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said not to pray at the rising of the sun, for it rises between the two horns of Satan and every disbeliever prostrates to it, nor at its setting, for it sets between the two horns of Satan and every disbeliever prostrates to it, nor at midday, for that is when Hell is stoked. In another wording, when asked at which time prayer is disliked, he defined it from the appearance of dawn until the sun rises a spear or two, and from when the sun yellows until its setting. > **""Do not pray at the rising of the sun, for it rises between the two horns of Satan and every disbeliever prostrates to it. Do not pray at its setting, for it sets between the two horns of Satan and every disbeliever prostrates to it. Do not pray at midday, for it is when Hell is stoked. From when dawn appears until the sun rises a spear or two, and from when the sun yellows until its setting.""**"
"3350","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the two forbidden times of prayer, explaining the reason connected with the Shaytan. > **""Do not pray when the sun is rising nor when it is setting, for it rises between the two horns of the Shaytan and sets between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3351","Salamah ibn al-Akwa' رضي الله عنه reports that he travelled in the company of the Prophet ﷺ and never once observed him offering any voluntary prayer after the Asr prayer or after the morning prayer."
"3352","Sa'id ibn Nafi' reports that Abu Bashir al-Ansari رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, saw him praying Salat al-Duha at the moment the sun rose, so he reproached him and forbade him, citing the Prophet's ﷺ command to delay prayer until the sun has risen high because of its rising between the horns of the Shaytan. > **""Do not pray until the sun has risen high, for it rises between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3353","Sa'id ibn Nafi' reports that Abu al-Yusr رضي الله عنه saw him praying Salat al-Duha and forbade him, citing the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ prohibition of praying while the sun is still low at its rising. > **""Do not pray when the sun is rising high, for it rises between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3354","Bilal رضي الله عنه reports that prayer was not forbidden except at the rising of the sun, giving as the reason its emergence between the horns of the Shaytan."
"3355","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people while leaning his back against the Ka'bah and forbade any prayer after Asr until the sun has set. > **""There is no prayer after Asr until the sun has set.""**"
"3356","Hayy ibn Ya'la ibn Umayyah reports that he saw Ya'la رضي الله عنه praying before sunrise, whereupon a man objected that a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ should pray before sunrise. Ya'la replied that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the sun as rising between the horns of the Shaytan, and added that for it to rise while one is engaged in Allah's command is better than for it to rise while one is heedless. > **""The sun rises between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3357","Abdullah ibn Rabah reports from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed Asr, a man stood to pray, so Umar رضي الله عنه saw him and told him to sit, explaining that the People of the Book were destroyed because their prayer had no separation."
"3358","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stated that the sun rises between the two horns of the Shaytan, and the narrator adds that they were therefore forbidden to pray at sunrise, at sunset, and at midday. > **""The sun rises, when it rises, between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3359","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade prayer at sunrise and at sunset, explaining that it rises and sets upon the horn of the Shaytan, and permitted prayer between those two times as one wishes. > **""Do not pray at the rising of the sun nor at its setting, for it sets and rises upon the horn of the Shaytan; pray between that as you wish.""**"
"3360","The narration reports that the Prophet ﷺ forbade prayer after Asr until the sun has set, and after Fajr until the sun has risen."
"3361","A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid prayer at the rising of the sun until it had risen high, stating that it rises with the horn of the Shaytan, and he likewise forbade prayer when it draws near to setting. > **""It rises with the horn of the Shaytan.""**"
"3362","Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked which part of the night is most heard, and he replied that it is the last depth of the night, after which he detailed when prayer is accepted and when it must be avoided: accepted until the appearance of Fajr, then no prayer until the sun stands a spear or two spears high, then accepted until the shadow stands like a spear, then no prayer until the sun declines, then accepted until the sun remains only a spear or two, then no prayer until it has set. > **""The last depth of the night; then prayer is accepted until Fajr appears, then there is no prayer until the sun stands the measure of a spear or two spears, then prayer is accepted until the shadow stands the measure of a spear, then there is no prayer until the sun declines, then prayer is accepted until the sun remains the measure of a spear or two, then there is no prayer until the sun has set.""**"
"3363","Safwan ibn al-Mu'attal al-Sulami رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ described how the Shaytan draws near the sun when it rises and parts from it once it has spread, draws near again when it approaches the zenith and parts once it has declined, then draws near when it approaches setting and parts once it has set, and on that account he forbade prayer during those hours. > **""When the sun rises the Shaytan draws alongside it, and when it has spread he parts from it; when it draws near the zenith he draws alongside it, and when it has declined he parts from it; when it draws near setting he draws alongside it, and when it has set he parts from it.""**"
"3364","Abu Usayd رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that there is no prayer after the Asr prayer. > **""There is no prayer after the Asr prayer.""**"
"3365","Kurayb reports that Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما, al-Miswar ibn Makhramah رضي الله عنهما, and Abdur Rahman ibn Azhar رضي الله عنه all stated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade prayer after the Asr prayer."
"3366","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they were forbidden to pray at the rising of the sun and at its setting. > **""We were forbidden to pray at the rising of the sun and at its setting.""**"
"3367","Qabisah Abu al-Muhallab reports from his father that the Prophet ﷺ was asked whether any hour of time withholds them from prayer, and he replied that there is none except at sunrise and sunset, for it rises and sets between the two horns of the Shaytan. > **""No, except at the rising of the sun and at its setting, for it rises between the two horns of the Shaytan and sets between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3368","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه stated that the sun rises between the two horns of the Shaytan and has scarcely risen the length of a reed before a gate of Hell is opened for it, and that at midday the gates of Hell are opened for it, and the narrator adds that Abdullah used to forbid prayer during these two times: from rising until it is high, and at midday."
"3369","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade prayer during three hours: at the rising of the sun until it has risen, at midday, and at the setting of the sun. > **""He forbade prayer during three hours: at the rising of the sun until it has risen, at midday, and at the setting of the sun.""**"
"3370","Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the mosque while the Prophet ﷺ was praying, and after the Prophet ﷺ gave the Salam he turned toward him while Thabit was praying. The Prophet ﷺ kept looking at him until he finished, then asked whether he had not prayed with them and what this prayer was. Thabit replied that they were the two Rakahs of Fajr which he had not prayed, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ found no fault in that. > **""Did you not pray with us?""**"
"3371","Wathilah رضي الله عنه reports that a questioner asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ why the call to prayer on Friday occurs at midday though he had forbidden it on other days, and he replied that Allah stokes Hell every day at midday but leaves it unstoked on Friday. > **""Allah, Mighty and Majestic, kindles Hell every day at midday, and leaves it unkindled on Friday.""**"
"3372","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه, having taken hold of the ring of the door of the Ka'bah, reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say there is no prayer after Asr until sunset nor after Fajr until sunrise, except in Makkah. > **""There is no prayer after Asr until the sun has set, nor after Fajr until the sun has risen, except in Makkah.""**"
"3373","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the Banu Abd Manaf should never be found preventing anyone from circumambulating the House and praying at whatever hour of night or day he wishes. > **""O Banu Abd Manaf, let me never find you preventing anyone from circumambulating the House and praying at whatever hour he wishes, by night or by day.""**"
"3374","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ addressed the Banu Abd Manaf and the Banu Abd al-Muttalib, commanding that if they are entrusted with this affair they must not forbid anyone who circumambulates this House from praying at whatever hour of night or day he wishes. > **""O Banu Abd Manaf, O Banu Abd al-Muttalib, if you are entrusted with this affair, do not forbid anyone who circumambulates this House from praying at whatever hour he wishes, by night or by day.""**"
"3375","Amr ibn Dinar reports that he saw Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما circumambulate after the morning prayer and then pray two Rakahs, explaining that prayer is disliked at the rising of the sun because the Prophet ﷺ said it rises between the two horns of the Shaytan. > **""The sun rises between the two horns of the Shaytan.""**"
"3376","Zirr ibn Hubaysh reports that he kept company with Ubayy ibn Ka'b رضي الله عنه and Abdur Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه, and he asserted that both of them used to stand when the sun had set and pray two Rakahs before Maghrib."
"3377","Ubayd, the freedman of the Prophet ﷺ, was asked about the prayer of the Prophet ﷺ, and he mentioned a prayer between Maghrib and Isha."
"3378","In another narration from Ubayd, he was asked whether the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to command prayer after the prescribed prayer or apart from the prescribed prayer, and he replied in the affirmative, specifying the time between Maghrib and Isha."
"3379","Mahmud ibn Labid رضي الله عنه, of the Banu Abd al-Ashhal, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to their mosque and led them in Maghrib, and when he gave the Salam he commanded them to pray these two Rakahs in their houses, meaning the optional prayer after Maghrib. > **""Pray these two Rakahs in your houses, for the optional prayer after Maghrib.""**"
"3380","Muhammad ibn Ammar ibn Yasir reports through his father from his grandfather that he saw Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه praying six Rakahs after Maghrib and asked him about this prayer. Ammar replied that he had seen his beloved, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, pray six Rakahs after Maghrib, and that he said whoever does so will have his sins forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea. > **""Whoever prays six Rakahs after Maghrib, his sins will be forgiven even if they are like the foam of the sea.""**"
"3381","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray two Rakahs after Maghrib, making the recitation in them so long that the people of the mosque began to disperse."
"3382","al-Aswad ibn Yazid reports that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه said that the hour of heedlessness is an excellent hour, meaning prayer between Maghrib and Isha."
"3383","Abdur Rahman ibn Yazid reports that whenever he came to Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه at that hour he found him praying between Maghrib and Isha, and when he asked him about it Abdullah replied that it is the hour of heedlessness."
"3384","Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali reports that they asked Abdullah ibn Ja'far رضي الله عنهما to narrate only what he himself had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and seen of him, narrating nothing from another even if trustworthy, whereupon he narrated a long Hadith in which he stated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun and Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad in the two Rakahs before Fajr and the two Rakahs after Maghrib."
"3385","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said four Rakahs before Zuhr equal their like after Isha, and four after Isha equal their like on Laylat al-Qadr. > **""Four before Zuhr are like their equal after Isha, and four after Isha are like their equal on Laylat al-Qadr.""**"
"3386","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports in a Marfu' narration that the Prophet ﷺ said whoever prays four Rakahs after the last Isha, reciting Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun and Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad in the first two and Tanzil al-Sajdah and Tabarak Alladhi bi-Yadihi al-Mulk in the last two, they will be recorded for him like four Rakahs of Laylat al-Qadr. > **""Whoever prays four Rakahs after the last Isha, reciting in the first two Qul Ya Ayyuha al-Kafirun and Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad, and in the last two Tanzil al-Sajdah and Tabarak Alladhi bi-Yadihi al-Mulk, they will be recorded for him like four Rakahs of Laylat al-Qadr.""**"
"3387","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever prays the last Isha in congregation and prays four Rakahs before leaving the mosque, it will be like the equal of Laylat al-Qadr. > **""Whoever prays the last Isha in congregation and prays four Rakahs before leaving the mosque, it will be like the equal of Laylat al-Qadr.""**"
"3388","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever prays twelve Rakahs in a day and night apart from the obligatory prayer, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise. > **""Whoever prays in a day and night twelve Rakahs apart from the obligatory prayer, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise.""**"
"3389","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ used to pray eight Rakahs of voluntary prayer by night and twelve Rakahs by day."
"3390","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that he prayed with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for ten years, and his prayer each day was ten Rakahs: two before Fajr, two before Zuhr and two after it, two after Maghrib, and two after Isha."
"3391","Buraydah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said there is a prayer between every two calls to prayer, except Maghrib. > **""Between every two calls there is a prayer, except Maghrib.""**"
"3392","Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said there is no prescribed prayer but that two Rakahs precede it. > **""There is no prescribed prayer but that two Rakahs precede it.""**"
"3393","Abu Ubaydah reports that the daytime prayer of Abdullah رضي الله عنه was four before Zuhr and two after it, two after Maghrib, two after Isha, and two before Fajr, and that he would not pray before Asr nor after it."
"3394","Yahya ibn Abi Kathir reports that Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah wrote to him describing the guidance, speech, and practice of Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه concerning Salah. He stated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was given concise speech and taught them what to say in prayer, then set out Ibn Mas'ud's beloved supplication after the Tashahhud, his insistence on humility and brevity in sitting, and his full daily practice: four at midmorning with long and short Surahs, then Zuhr with prolonged standing in the first two Rakahs, two after it, Asr while the day is still long with shorter recitation and no prayer after it until sunset, Maghrib, which they call Isha, with short Mufassal chapters followed by two Rakahs about whose time he would swear by Allah, then the morning prayer, which they call Subh, at which the two guards gather and beside which he disliked all speech but dhikr and Quran, then the night Isha, which they call Atamah, with the endings of Al Imran and al-Furqan in measured, beautiful recitation, two Rakahs after every prescribed prayer and four after Jumu'ah, Witr late at night, and Fajr at the splitting of dawn with long chapters, with Takbir at every movement, Tasbih while standing and sitting, and two Salams without turning or gesturing. > **""Al-Tahiyyatu Lillah, wa al-Salawatu al-Tayyibat; peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings; peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. Then ask whatever occurs to you thereafter, seeking His mercy and forgiveness with brief words, and do not prolong the sitting. Glory be to You, there is no god besides You; forgive my sin and set right my deed, for You forgive sins for whom You will, and You are the Forgiving, the Merciful. O Oft-Forgiving, forgive me; O Oft-Returning, accept my repentance; O Merciful, have mercy on me; O Pardoning, pardon me; O Gentle, show gentleness to me. My Lord, inspire me to thank the blessing You have bestowed upon me, clothe me with the excellence of Your worship, I ask You for all good and seek refuge in You from all evil, open for me with good and seal for me with good, grant me longing to meet You without harmful adversity or misguiding trial, shield me from evil deeds, for whom You shield from evil deeds on that Day, You have had mercy on him, and that is the supreme triumph.""**"
"3395","A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ used to follow every prayer with two Rakahs, except the morning prayer, for which he placed them before it."
"3396","Masruq reports that he asked A'ishah رضي الله عنها about the voluntary prayer of the Prophet ﷺ while travelling, and she replied that it was two Rakahs after every prayer."
"3397","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that his close friend ﷺ counselled him to pray the two Rakahs of Fajr for their enduring merits, the two of Duha as the prayer of the penitent, two before Zuhr and two after it, two after Asr, two after Maghrib, and two after Isha, to fast three days of each month as the fast of eternity, never to sleep without Witr, and to pray two Rakahs at the start of the day with his guarantee for its end. > **""It is the fast of eternity. O Abu Hurayrah, pray two Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I guarantee its end for you.""**"
"3398","Abdullah ibn Rabah reports from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ that after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed Asr a man stood to pray, so Umar رضي الله عنه told him to sit, explaining that the People of the Book were destroyed because their prayer had no separation, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ approved his words. > **""Ibn al-Khattab spoke well.""**"
"3399","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he never saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray Duha at all, except when he was setting out on a journey or returning from one."
"3400","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray Duha only once."
"3401","Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reports that his father never used to pray Salat al-Duha."
"3402","Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf reports that the first to pray Duha was a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ known by the Kunyah Abu al-Zawa'id."
"3403","A'ishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Prophet ﷺ never prayed Duha except on the day of the conquest of Makkah."
"3404","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to pray from the Duha prayer."
"3405","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an expedition which gained booty and returned swiftly, and the people spoke of its near field, abundant booty, and quick return. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then offered to guide them to a nearer field, greater booty, and quicker return: one who performs Wudu and then goes to the mosque in the morning for Salat al-Duha. > **""Shall I not guide you to one nearer in field, greater in booty, and quicker in return? Whoever performs Wudu and then goes to the mosque for the morning Duha prayer, he is nearer in field, greater in booty, and quicker in return.""**"
"3406","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an expedition which took great booty and returned swiftly, so a man marvelled that no expedition had returned so fast with so much. The Prophet ﷺ then offered to tell of one swifter in return and greater in booty: a man who performs Wudu well in his house, goes to the mosque, prays the morning prayer there, and then follows it with the Duha prayer. > **""Shall I not tell you of one swifter in return than it and greater in booty? A man who performs Wudu in his house and perfects it, then goes to the mosque, prays the morning prayer in it, and then follows it with the Duha prayer, he has returned more swiftly and taken greater booty.""**"
"3407","A'idh ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reports that water was scarce, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Wudu and sprinkled the remainder upon them, and they considered fortunate whoever among them was struck by it, believing it had reached them all. He then states that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ led them in the Duha prayer."
"3408","Itban ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ prayed the Duha optional prayer in his house."
"3409","Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says that if the son of Adam suffices Him at the start of the day with four Rakahs, He will suffice him with them at the end of his day. > **""O son of Adam, suffice Me at the beginning of the day with four Rakahs and I will suffice you with them at the end of your day.""**"
"3410","Abu al-Darda' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says the son of Adam should not fail to pray four Rakahs at the start of the day, for He will suffice him at its end. > **""Son of Adam, do not fail to pray four Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I will suffice you at its end.""**"
"3411","Abu Murrah al-Ta'ifi رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ convey that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says the son of Adam should pray four Rakahs for Him at the start of the day, and He will suffice him at its end. > **""Son of Adam, pray for Me four Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I will suffice you at its end.""**"
"3412","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah says if the son of Adam prays two Rakahs for Him at the start of the day, He guarantees him its end. > **""Allah says: Son of Adam, pray for Me two Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I guarantee you its end.""**"
"3413","al-Nawwas ibn Sam'an رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ convey that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says the son of Adam should not fall short of four Rakahs at the start of the day, and He will suffice him at its end. > **""Son of Adam, do not fall short of four Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I will suffice you at its end.""**"
"3414","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed that Allah says the son of Adam should bow for Him four Rakahs at the start of the day, and He will suffice him at its end. > **""Allah says: O son of Adam, bow for Me four Rakahs at the beginning of the day and I will suffice you at its end.""**"
"3415","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed eight Rakahs in Makkah on the day of its conquest, lengthening the recitation and the bowing in them."
"3416","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports that he went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ one day during the expedition of Tabuk, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sat speaking to his Companions, saying that whoever rises when the sun faces him, performs Wudu well, then stands and prays two Rakahs, his sins will be forgiven and he will be as his mother bore him. > **""Whoever rises when the sun faces him, performs Wudu and perfects it, then stands and prays two Rakahs, his sins will be forgiven and he will be as his mother bore him.""**"
"3417","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey pray eight Rakahs of Duha, and when he finished he explained that he had prayed a prayer of hope and fear and asked his Lord for three things, of which He granted two and withheld one: that He not try his Ummah with famine and not empower their enemy over them, which He granted, and that He not divide them into factions, which He refused. > **""I prayed a prayer of hope and fear, and I asked my Lord for three things: He granted me two and withheld one from me. I asked Him not to try my Ummah with famine and not to empower their enemy over them, so He did so, and I asked Him not to divide them into factions, but He refused me.""**"
"3418","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that he asked his uncle Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه for counsel, and Abu Dharr replied that he had asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about the same matter. The Prophet ﷺ explained the ranks of Duha: two Rakahs keep one from the heedless, four record one among the worshippers, six suffice, eight record one among the devoutly obedient, and twelve build a house in Paradise; and he added that in every day, night, and hour Allah bestows a charity upon whom He wills of His servants, and no bestowal upon a servant is like inspiring him with His remembrance. > **""If you pray two Rakahs of Duha you will not be recorded among the heedless; if you pray four you will be recorded among the worshippers; if you pray six you will be sufficed; if you pray eight you will be recorded among the devoutly obedient; if you pray twelve Rakahs a house will be built for you in Paradise. There is no day, night, or hour but that Allah has therein a charity with which He favours whom He wills of His servants, and He has favoured no servant with anything like inspiring him with His remembrance.""**"
"3419","Abu al-Darda' رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said whoever prays two of Duha is not recorded among the heedless, whoever prays four is recorded among the worshippers, whoever prays six is sufficed that day, whoever prays eight Allah records among the devoutly obedient, and whoever prays twelve Allah builds for him a house in Paradise; and in every day and night Allah bestows favour and charity upon His servants, with no favour upon any servant better than inspiring him with His remembrance. > **""Whoever prays two of Duha will not be recorded among the heedless; whoever prays four will be recorded among the worshippers; whoever prays six will be sufficed that day; whoever prays eight, Allah will record him among the devoutly obedient; whoever prays twelve, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise. There is no day or night but that Allah has a favour with which He favours His servants, and charity, and Allah has favoured no servant of His with anything better than inspiring him with His remembrance.""**"
"3420","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports in a Marfu' narration that the Prophet ﷺ said a charity is due for every joint of the son of Adam each day, and the two Rakahs of Duha suffice for all of that. > **""Upon every joint of the son of Adam each day there is a charity, and the two Rakahs of Duha suffice for all of that.""**"
"3421","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said when the sun has risen from its place of rising to the height it holds at Asr time when setting from its place of setting, and a man prays two Rakahs with four prostrations, he earns the reward of that day, his sins and faults are expiated, and if he dies that day he will enter Paradise. > **""When the sun has risen from its place of rising to the height of the Asr prayer when it sets from its place of setting, and a man prays two Rakahs with four prostrations, he earns the reward of that day, his sin and fault are expiated for him, and if he dies that day he will enter Paradise.""**"
"3422","Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه reports that he saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pray six Rakahs of Duha, and he never abandoned them thereafter."
"3423","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reports that he was cut off while travelling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so the Prophet ﷺ mounted him upon a camel. When the Prophet ﷺ passed by him while he was at the rear beating his camel, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ struck it with his whip and it moved to the foremost of the people. When they arrived in Makkah, Jabir came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to return the camel to him and found him praying six Rakahs."
"3424","In another narration Jabir رضي الله عنهما reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to present his camel to him and saw him praying six Rakahs of Duha."
"3425","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the reward for one who prays four rak'ahs in the forenoon and four before the first obligatory prayer, and that a house in Paradise is built for him. > **""Whoever prays Duha four rak'ahs and four before the first prayer, a house will be built for him in Paradise.""**"
"3426","Jubayr ibn Mut'im رضي الله عنه reported that he saw the Prophet ﷺ praying the Duha prayer."
"3427","Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه prayed two rak'ahs of Duha, whereupon his wife asked him whether he had prayed only two rak'ahs, and he explained that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed it as two rak'ahs when he was given glad tidings of the conquest and when he was given glad tidings of the head of Abu Jahl."
"3428","Umm Hani رضي الله عنها reported that on the day of the conquest of Makkah the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called for water, and Umm Hani and Umm Sulaym, the mother of Anas ibn Malik رضي الله عنه, screened him with a sheet, then he entered the house of Umm Hani and prayed Duha as four rak'ahs."
"3429","Umm Hani رضي الله عنها reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered upon her on the day of the conquest and prayed Duha as six rak'ahs."
"3430","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that he used to pass by the verse concerning glorification in the evening and at sunrise without knowing its meaning, until Umm Hani bint Abi Talib رضي الله عنها informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered upon her and called for ablution water in a large bowl in which the trace of dough could still be seen, performed ablution, then prayed Duha, and then addressed Umm Hani concerning the nature of that prayer. > **""O Umm Hani, this is the Ishraq prayer.""**"
"3431","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not abandon the Duha prayer while travelling nor otherwise."
"3432","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the one who alone maintains the Duha prayer with constancy. > **""None preserves the Duha prayer except an Awwab.""**"
"3433","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning a gate in Paradise named Duha, and that on the Day of Resurrection a caller will summon those who maintained the Duha prayer to enter through their gate by the mercy of Allah. > **""Indeed in Paradise there is a gate called Duha. When the Day of Resurrection comes, a caller will call: Where are those who used to persist in the Duha prayer? This is your gate, so enter it by the mercy of Allah.""**"
"3434","Ibrahim reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه was asked about a man who lays his side down at the time of the two rak'ahs of Duha, and he censured the conduct by likening it to wallowing. > **""What is the matter with one of you wallowing as a donkey wallows?""**"
"3435","Abu Tamim al-Jayshani reported that he heard Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه say that a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, namely Abu Basrah al-Ghifari رضي الله عنه, informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced an additional prayer to be performed between Isha and Fajr, namely Witr. Abu Tamim added that he was once sitting with Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه, who took him by the hand and went with him to Abu Basrah at the door adjoining the door of Amr, where Abu Dharr asked him whether he himself had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ utter that announcement concerning the added prayer between Isha and the morning prayer, and Abu Basrah affirmed it twice when asked whether he had heard it directly. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has added a prayer for you, so perform it between Isha and Fajr: Witr, Witr.""**"
"3436","Abdurrahman ibn Rafi' al-Tanukhi reported that Mu'adh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه came to Sham while its people did not perform Witr, so he asked Mu'awiyah رضي الله عنه why he saw the people of Sham neglecting Witr, and Mu'awiyah asked whether it was binding upon them, to which Mu'adh replied affirmatively and cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about his Lord granting an additional prayer. > **""My Lord, Mighty and Majestic, has granted me an additional prayer, and it is Witr, between Isha and the rising of dawn.""**"
"3437","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that Allah had granted an additional prayer and commanded its observance. > **""Indeed Allah has granted you an additional prayer, so guard it, and it is Witr.""**"
"3438","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared unlawful for his Ummah wine, gambling, mizr, the qinnin and the kubah, and announced that the Witr prayer had been granted to him in addition. > **""Indeed Allah has forbidden wine, gambling, mizr, the qinnin and the kubah for my Ummah, and He has granted me the Witr prayer in addition.""**"
"3439","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ warned concerning abandonment of Witr. > **""Whoever does not perform Witr is not of us.""**"
"3440","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the obligation of Witr. > **""Witr is obligatory upon every Muslim.""**"
"3441","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out with joy evident upon his face and announced that Allah had granted an additional prayer. > **""Indeed Allah has granted you an additional prayer, and it is Witr.""**"
"3442","Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنه and Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Allah had granted an additional prayer better for them than red camels, to be performed in the interval between Isha and the rise of dawn. > **""Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has granted you an additional prayer, better for you than red camels: Witr, and it is between the Isha prayer and the rising of dawn.""**"
"3443","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه conveyed as a Marfu' report that Witr is obligatory upon every Muslim, with concession according to ability from five to three to one, and to gesturing for one unable even to perform one. > **""Witr is obligatory upon every Muslim. Whoever is able to perform Witr with five, let him do so with five; whoever is unable to do so with five, let him do so with three; whoever is unable to do so with three, let him do so with one; and whoever is unable to do so with one, let him gesture with a gesture.""**"
"3444","Abu Ayyub al-Ansari رضي الله عنه is reported to have conveyed that Witr is an established right and that one who wishes may perform it as seven, with the remainder of the report as previously narrated. > **""Witr is a right, so whoever wishes may perform Witr with seven.""**"
"3445","Abdullah ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ declared that Allah, Blessed and Exalted, is Witr and loves that which is odd-numbered, and Nafi' added that Ibn Umar would do nothing except in odd number. > **""Indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, is Witr and loves Witr.""**"
"3446","Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ laid Witr upon the people of the Quran. > **""Witr is upon the people of the Quran.""**"
"3447","Sulayman ibn Surad رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ commanded the use of the tooth-stick, cleanliness, and the performance of Witr, on the ground that Allah is Witr and loves Witr. > **""Use the tooth-stick, keep clean, and perform Witr, for indeed Allah is Witr and loves Witr.""**"
"3448","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise the reward of a martyr for one who prays Duha, fasts three days of the month, and never abandons Witr in travel or residence. > **""Whoever prays Duha, fasts three days of the month, and does not abandon Witr in travel or residence, the reward of a martyr will be recorded for him.""**"
"3449","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ would perform Witr as nine rak'ahs, until when his body grew heavy and his flesh increased he performed Witr as seven, and prayed two rak'ahs sitting in which he recited When the earth is shaken and Say: O disbelievers, with the addition of Say: He is Allah, the One."
"3450","Abu Ayyub رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed performance of Witr as five, then three if unable, then one if unable, then gesturing if unable even for one. > **""Perform Witr with five; if you are unable, then with three; if you are unable, then with one; if you are unable, then gesture with a gesture.""**"
"3451","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that he spent the night with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and when the first dawn appeared he rose and performed Witr as nine rak'ahs, giving salam after every two rak'ahs."
"3452","Ibn Abi Awfa رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform Witr as three rak'ahs, reciting in the first Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, in the second Say: O disbelievers, and in the third Say: He is Allah, the One, and when he gave salam he would say Glory be to the Sovereign, the Most Holy while prolonging his voice with it. > **""Subhan al-Malik al-Quddus.""**"
"3453","Abdullah ibn Babi reported that he came to Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما at Arafah and saw that he had pitched one tent in the Hill and one in the Haram. When asked why he had done so, he explained that his prayer would thus be in the Haram while his return to his family would be in the Hill. When asked how he performed Witr, he declared seven most beloved to him, citing that Allah created seven heavens, seven earths, and seven days, made Tawaf seven circuits, Sa'y between Safa and Marwah seven circuits, and the stoning seven pebbles, and then added that Allah created nothing on earth from Paradise except this ruby, the Black Stone, and that by Allah it will surely be raised before the Day of Resurrection. > **""The most beloved Witr to me is seven.""**"
"3454","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened Witr to the Maghrib prayer in number. > **""Witr is three, like the three of Maghrib.""**"
"3455","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه stated that the Witr of the night is like the Witr of the day, namely the Maghrib prayer of three rak'ahs."
"3456","Abu Ubaydah reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه would perform Witr as three rak'ahs and upward."
"3457","Husayn reported that it reached Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه that Sa'd رضي الله عنه performed Witr as one rak'ah, whereupon he declared that a single rak'ah never suffices. > **""One rak'ah has never sufficed.""**"
"3458","Ibrahim reported that Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه said to Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه in astonishment whether he performed Witr as one, and Sa'd asked whether Witr is not one, to which Abdullah replied affirmatively but maintained that three is superior. Sa'd insisted that he would not add to it, so Abdullah grew angry, whereupon Sa'd asked whether he was angry with him for performing Witr as one rak'ah while he himself gave inheritance to three grandmothers, asking why he did not then give inheritance to Hawwa, the wife of Adam. > **""Yes, but three is superior.""**"
"3459","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ performed Witr as one rak'ah."
"3460","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ performed Witr as one rak'ah."
"3461","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray by night two by two, and when morning approached he would perform Witr as one rak'ah, declaring that Allah is One and loves that which is one. > **""Indeed Allah is One and loves that which is one.""**"
"3462","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray in the chamber while she was in the house, and he would separate the even prayer from Witr by a salam which he let them hear."
"3463","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would separate the even prayer from Witr by a salutation which he let them hear."
"3464","Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite in Witr in the first rak'ah Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, in the second Say: O disbelievers, and in the third Say: He is Allah, the One."
"3465","Al-Nu'man ibn Bashir رضي الله عنه reported that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with what he should perform Witr, and the Prophet ﷺ named the three chapters for the three rak'ahs. > **""With: Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, and Say: O disbelievers, and Say: He is Allah, the One.""**"
"3466","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that in Witr he would recite in the first rak'ah Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, in the second Say: O disbelievers, and in the third Say: He is Allah, the One along with the two chapters of refuge."
"3467","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported from the Prophet ﷺ that in Witr he would recite Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, Say: O disbelievers, and Say: He is Allah, the One."
"3468","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that in Witr he would recite Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High, Say: O disbelievers, and Say: He is Allah, the One."
"3469","Abdurrahman ibn Sabrah, meaning Abu Khaythamah, reported that his father asked the Prophet ﷺ what to recite in Witr, and he instructed recitation of the three chapters in order across the three rak'ahs. In another narration he said that he and his father entered upon the Prophet ﷺ and the like was mentioned. > **""Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High in the first, and in the second: Say: O disbelievers, and Say: He is Allah, the One in the third.""**"
"3470","Al-Husayn ibn Ali رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught him words to say in the Qunut of Witr, comprising supplication for guidance, well-being, guardianship, blessing, and protection from the evil of what has been decreed, with affirmation that He decrees and none decrees against Him, and that one befriended by Him is never abased. > **""O Allah, guide me among those You have guided, grant me well-being among those You have granted well-being, befriend me among those You have befriended, bless for me what You have given, and shield me from the evil of what You have decreed. Indeed You decree and none decrees against You, and indeed one whom You befriend is never abased. Blessed are You, our Lord, and Exalted.""**"
"3471","Al-Aswad reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه would recite in the last rak'ah of Witr Say: He is Allah, the One, then raise his hands and perform Qunut before bowing."
"3472","Al-Nakha'i reported that Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه would perform Qunut in Witr throughout the entire year."
"3473","Abdurrahman ibn al-Aswad reported that Abdullah رضي الله عنه would not perform Qunut in his obligatory prayers, but when he performed Qunut in Witr he did so before bowing."
"3474","Abu Mas'ud Uqbah ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he would perform Witr at the beginning of the night, its middle, and its end, with the addition that whichever of these one does is correct."
"3475","Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas رضي الله عنه would pray the last Isha in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and then perform Witr as one rak'ah without adding to it. When it was said to him in astonishment whether he performed Witr as one without addition, he affirmed it and cited what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning one who does not sleep until he has performed Witr. > **""He who does not sleep until he performs Witr is resolute.""**"
"3476","Ali رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade him to sleep except upon Witr. > **""Do not sleep except upon Witr.""**"
"3477","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ asked Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه how he performed Witr, and he replied that he performed it at the beginning of the night, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ described him as cautious and discerning. He then asked Umar رضي الله عنه how he performed Witr, and he replied from the end of the night, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ described him as strong and aided. > **""Cautious, discerning.""** > **""Strong, aided.""**"
"3478","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه when he performed Witr, and he replied that he prayed two by two and then performed Witr before sleeping, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ called him a cautious believer. He then asked Umar رضي الله عنه how he performed Witr, and he replied that he prayed two by two, then slept until he performed Witr at the end of the night, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ called him a strong believer. > **""A cautious believer.""** > **""A strong believer.""**"
"3479","Uqbah ibn Amr رضي الله عنه and Abu Musa رضي الله عنه both reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would sometimes perform Witr at the beginning of the night and its middle so that there would be latitude for the Muslims."
"3480","Thuwayr ibn Abi Faktah reported from a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ called Abu al-Khattab رضي الله عنه that he asked the Prophet ﷺ about Witr, and the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he would love to perform Witr at midnight, explaining that Allah descends from the highest heaven to the lowest heaven and calls for any asker, seeker of forgiveness, and supplicant until dawn rises, when He ascends. > **""Would you love for me to perform Witr at midnight? Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, descends from the highest heaven to the lowest heaven and says: Is there any asker? Is there any seeker of forgiveness? Is there any supplicant? Until when dawn rises, He ascends.""**"
"3481","Alqamah reported that a man came to Abdullah رضي الله عنه and asked when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would perform Witr, and he replied that it was when there remained of the night about as much as had passed from it up to the Maghrib prayer. When they asked about his recitation, he said that he would let the people of the house hear it."
"3482","Al-Aswad ibn Hilal testified concerning Abdullah ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه that he heard him proclaim aloud that Witr is between the two prayers, the last Isha which they call Atamah and the Fajr prayer, and that whenever one performs Witr therein it is good. > **""Witr is between the two prayers. Whenever you perform Witr, it is good.""**"
"3483","Abd Khayr reported that they were in the mosque when Ali رضي الله عنه came out to them at the end of the night and asked where the questioner about Witr was. They gathered to him, and he informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Witr at the beginning of the night, then in its middle, then at this hour, and that he passed away while performing Witr at this hour. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Witr at the beginning of the night, then he performed Witr in its middle, then he performed Witr at this hour, and he passed away while performing Witr at this hour.""**"
"3484","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه would go out when Ibn al-Tayyah called the adhan at the first dawn and declare it an excellent hour for Witr, interpreting thereby the verse By the morning when it breathes. > **""What an excellent hour for Witr is this.""**"
"3485","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما, when asked about Witr, explained that if he had performed Witr before sleeping and then wished to pray at night, he would add one rak'ah to make his prior Witr even, then pray two by two, and when he finished his prayer he would perform Witr with one, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded that the last prayer of the night be made Witr. > **""Make the last of your prayer by night Witr.""**"
"3486","Ata ibn al-Saib reported from more than one of the companions of Abdullah that Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه used to say that when one of them has performed Witr and then slept and risen, he should annul his Witr by adding another rak'ah to it, then perform Witr again afterwards. > **""When one of you performs Witr and then sleeps and rises, let him make up his Witr by praying another with it, then let him perform Witr after that.""**"
"3487","Thawban رضي الله عنه reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey when he explained that the journey is toil and burden, and instructed that when one of them has performed Witr he should pray two rak'ahs, so that if he awakes it is well, and otherwise they stand for him. > **""Indeed this journey is toil and burden. When one of you performs Witr, let him bow two rak'ahs; if he awakes, well and good, otherwise they will be for him.""**"
"3488","Al-Agharr al-Muzani رضي الله عنه reported from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one whom morning overtakes without having performed Witr. > **""Whoever is overtaken by morning without having performed Witr, there is no Witr for him.""**"
"3489","Al-Agharr al-Muzani رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that morning had come upon him while he had not performed Witr, and the Prophet ﷺ replied that Witr belongs to the night. When the man repeated his plea, the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to perform it. > **""Witr is only by night.""** > **""Then perform Witr.""**"
"3490","Abu Nuhayk reported that Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه was addressing the people that there is no Witr for one whom morning overtakes, so a group of believers went to Aishah رضي الله عنها and informed her, and she replied that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would enter the morning and then perform Witr."
"3491","Abu Sa'id al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that it was said to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether Witr may follow the dawn adhan, and he twice instructed that Witr be performed before the adhan, the adhan of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ being after the rise of dawn. When they asked a third time, he permitted Witr after the adhan as a concession. > **""Perform Witr before the adhan.""** > **""Perform Witr after the adhan.""**"
"3492","Urwah ibn al-Zubayr reported that Ibn Mas'ud رضي الله عنه would perform Witr after dawn, while his father al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه would perform Witr before dawn."
"3493","Urwah ibn Mas'ud stated that he would not mind if the call for the Fajr prayer were made while he was still engaged in his nightly portion and had not yet performed Witr. > **""I do not mind if the call for the Fajr prayer is made while I am in my nightly portion and have not yet performed Witr.""**"
"3494","Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded prayer in houses and forbade taking them as graves. > **""Pray in your houses and do not take them as graves.""**"
"3495","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to enjoin prayer in houses and forbid making them as graves over oneself. > **""Pray in your houses and do not make them graves over you.""**"
"3496","Suhayb ibn al-Nu'man رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ likened the superiority of a man's prayer in his house over his prayer where people see him to the superiority of the obligatory prayer over the voluntary. > **""The superiority of a man's prayer in his house over his prayer where people see him is like the superiority of the obligatory prayer over the voluntary.""**"
"3497","Al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded prayer in houses without taking them as graves, forbade taking his house as a festival, and commanded sending blessings and peace upon him, for such blessings and peace reach him wherever one may be. > **""Pray in your houses and do not take them as graves, and do not take my house as a festival. Send blessings and peace upon me, for your blessings and peace reach me wherever you are.""**"
"3498","Aishah رضي الله عنها reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ conveyed the word of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, that whoever harms His ally has made war with Him lawful, that His servant draws near to Him with nothing like the obligatory deeds, and that the servant continues to draw near through voluntary deeds until He loves him, so that if he asks Him He gives him and if he calls upon Him He answers him, and that He does not hesitate concerning anything He does as He hesitates concerning the death of the believer, for he dislikes death and He dislikes grieving him, with the addition that when He loves him He becomes his eye with which he sees, his ear with which he hears, his hand with which he grasps, and his foot with which he walks. > **""Whoever harms a saint of Mine has made war with Me lawful. My servant does not draw near to Me with the like of the obligatory deeds, and My servant continues to draw near to Me with voluntary deeds until I love him. If he asks Me, I shall give him; if he calls upon Me, I shall answer him. And I have not hesitated concerning anything I do as I hesitate concerning his death, for he dislikes death and I dislike grieving him.""**"
"3499","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that Allah, Exalted is He, declares that His servant continues to draw near to Him through voluntary deeds until He loves him, whereupon He becomes his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees, his tongue with which he speaks, and his heart with which he understands, so that when he calls upon Him He answers him, when he asks Him He gives him, and when he seeks His aid He aids him, and that the most beloved act by which His servant worships Him is sincere counsel toward Him. > **""My servant continues to draw near to Me with voluntary deeds until I love him. I become his hearing with which he hears, his sight with which he sees, his tongue with which he speaks, and his heart with which he understands. When he calls upon Me, I answer him; when he asks Me, I give him; when he seeks My aid, I aid him. And the most beloved act by which My servant worships Me is sincere devotion to Me.""**"
"3500","In another narration from the Prophet ﷺ it is reported that Allah Most High declares enmity against whoever humiliates His beloved servant, and that the son of Adam attains what is with Him only by fulfilling what He has made obligatory, while His servant continues to draw near to Him through supererogatory acts until He loves him, then the narrator mentioned the rest with the same meaning. > **""Whoever humiliates a Wali of Mine has openly declared war against Me. O son of Adam, you will never attain what is with Me except by fulfilling what I have made obligatory upon you, and My servant continues to seek My love through supererogatory deeds until I love him.""**"
"3501","Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ went out in winter while leaves were falling in gusts, so he took hold of a branch of a tree and its leaves began to fall, then he called out to him and explained that the Muslim servant who prays seeking thereby the Face of Allah has his sins fall away just as these leaves fall from this tree. > **""O Abu Dharr. Indeed the Muslim servant performs the prayer seeking thereby the Face of Allah, so his sins fall away from him just as these leaves fall from this tree.""**"
"3502","Mutarrif reports that he sat with a group of Quraysh when a man came and began to pray with bowing and prostration without sitting, so he remarked that the man seemed heedless of whether he would finish upon an even or odd number, and the people urged him to admonish him. He thus approached him and questioned him, but the man replied that Allah knows, and informed him that he had heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise a good deed recorded, a sin effaced, and a rank raised for every prostration for Allah. When asked who he was, he replied that he was Abu Dharr رضي الله عنه, whereupon Mutarrif returned to his companions and reproached them for ordering him to teach a Companion of the Prophet ﷺ. In another narration he saw him prolonging the standing and increasing bowing and prostration, and when he mentioned it the man replied that he was careful to excel, having heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ promise elevation in rank and removal of sin for every bowing and prostration. > **""Whoever prostrates to Allah a single prostration, Allah records for him thereby a good deed, effaces thereby a sin, and raises thereby a rank.""**"
"3503","Ziyad ibn Abi Ziyad, freedman of Banu Makhzum, reports from a servant of the Prophet ﷺ, whether a man or a woman, that the Prophet ﷺ used to ask the servant whether he had any need, until one day the servant replied that he did have a need. When asked what it was, the servant asked for his intercession on the Day of Resurrection. The Prophet ﷺ asked who had directed him to this, and the servant replied that his Lord Almighty had, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ consented but commanded him to assist in that through abundant prostration. > **""If it must be so, then help me for yourself through abundant prostration.""**"
"3504","Abu Fatimah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said to him that if he desired to meet him in the Hereafter, he should increase in prostration. > **""O Abu Fatimah, if you wish to meet me, then increase in prostration.""**"
"3505","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that prayer is the best of instituted deeds, so whoever is able to increase therein should do so. > **""Prayer is the best institution, so whoever is able to increase therein, let him increase.""**"
"3506","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a grave and asked who its occupant was. When the people named a certain man, he explained how precious even a brief prayer would now be to the deceased in comparison to all they still possess of the world. > **""Two rakahs are more beloved to this man than the remainder of your world.""**"
"3507","Jabir ibn Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that a youth used to serve the Prophet ﷺ while concealing his own needs, so the Prophet ﷺ invited him to ask for his need. The youth asked him to supplicate to Allah Almighty for Paradise for him. The narrator states that the Prophet ﷺ raised his head, breathed, and agreed, but directed him to assist through abundant prostration. > **""Yes, but help me for yourself through abundant prostration.""**"
"3508","Rabiah ibn Kab رضي الله عنه reports that he used to serve the Prophet ﷺ by day, and at night he would resort to the door of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and spend the night there, continually hearing him glorify Allah with Subhan Allah and Subhan Rabbi until weariness or sleep overcame him. One day the Prophet ﷺ invited him to ask so that he might be granted. He requested respite to reflect, then recalled that the world is transient and severed, and asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to supplicate to Allah to save him from the Fire and admit him into Paradise. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell silent, then asked who had instructed him in this, and he replied that no one had, but that he knew the world to be fleeting while the Prophet ﷺ holds an eminent station with Allah, so he desired his supplication. The Prophet ﷺ promised to do so but commanded him to assist through abundant prostration. > **""Indeed I shall do so, but help me for yourself through abundant prostration.""**"
"3509","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that he will be the first to be permitted to raise his head on the Day of Resurrection, and that he will then recognise his Ummah to his right and his left. When asked how he would recognise them, he explained that they will be radiant with the traces of prostration, and their offspring will be their light before them. > **""I am the first for whom permission will be granted to raise his head, so I shall raise my head and recognise my Ummah to my right and to my left. They are radiant with the traces of prostration, and their offspring, their light shall be before them.""**"
"3510","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said that no state in which Allah finds His servant is more beloved to Allah than finding him with his face laid in dust before Him in prostration."
"3511","Abu Rayhanah رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and complained to him that the Quran slips away from him and that its memorisation is burdensome upon him, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade him to burden himself with what he cannot bear and enjoined prostration upon him. > **""Do not burden yourself with what you cannot bear, and upon you is prostration.""**"
"3512","Jubayr ibn Nawfal reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah does not permit His servant anything more excellent than two rakahs or more, that righteousness showers over the head of the servant so long as he is in prayer, and that the servant draws nearest to Allah Almighty through nothing more excellent than what issued from Him, meaning the Quran. > **""Allah does not permit a servant anything more excellent than two rakahs or more, and righteousness scatters above the head of the servant so long as he is in prayer, and no servant draws nearer to Allah Almighty through anything more excellent than what issued from Him, meaning the Quran.""**"
"3513","Salamah ibn al-Akwa رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they should remain upright though they will never encompass all good, and know that the most excellent of their deeds is prayer, and that none preserves prayer but a believer. > **""Remain upright, though you will never encompass all, and know that the most excellent of your deeds is prayer, and none preserves prayer except a believer.""**"
"3514","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they should remain upright, and how excellent it is if they do so, and that the best of their deeds is prayer. > **""Remain upright, and how excellent it is if you remain upright, and the best of your deeds is prayer.""**"
"3515","Malik ibn Qays reports that Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه came to Muawiyah رضي الله عنه while he was in Ilya, then soon departed and was sought but not found, so they came to him and found him praying in an open tract of land. When he asked what had brought them, they replied that they had come to renew their bond with him and fulfil his right. He replied that he had with him their reward, then recounted that they were once with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey, and each man was charged with tending the camels for a day. When his own day came, he pastured the camels, then came to the Prophet ﷺ while his Companions gathered around him as he spoke. He neglected the camels and turned toward him, arriving as he was saying that whoever performs wudu excellently then prays two rakahs seeking thereby the Face of Allah will be forgiven what preceded. He exclaimed Allahu Akbar, whereupon a man struck his shoulder. He turned to find Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه, who told him that what the Prophet ﷺ had said before that was even more excellent. When he asked what it was, Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said that whoever testifies that none is worthy of worship except Allah, his heart confirming his tongue, shall enter through whichever gate of Paradise he wishes. > **""Whoever performs wudu and perfects the wudu, then prays two rakahs seeking thereby the Face of Allah, Allah forgives him what preceded. Whoever testifies that none is worthy of worship except Allah, his heart confirming his tongue, shall enter through whichever of the gates of Paradise he wishes.""**"
"3516","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say that the expiation of every heated dispute is two rakahs. > **""The expiation of every quarrel is two rakahs.""**"
"3517","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that standing in night prayer was prescribed upon them through the verses commanding the enwrapped one to stand the night except a little, so they stood until their feet swelled, whereupon Allah Blessed and Exalted sent down the concession at the end of the Surah, that He knows that some among them will be sick, until the end of the Surah."
"3518","Abdullah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the excellence of night prayer over day prayer is like the excellence of secret charity over open charity. > **""The excellence of night prayer over day prayer is like the excellence of secret charity over open charity.""**"
"3519","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that they must hold fast to standing in night prayer, for it was the practice of the righteous before them, a means of nearness to their Lord, an expiation for evil deeds, and a deterrent from sin. > **""Upon you is standing in night prayer, for it was the practice of the righteous before you, and it is nearness to your Lord, an expiation for evil deeds, and a deterrent from sin.""**"
"3520","Salman al-Farisi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that they must hold fast to standing in night prayer, for it was the practice of the righteous before them, a means of nearness to Allah Almighty, an expiation for evil deeds, a deterrent from sin, and a repeller of disease from the body. > **""Upon you is standing in night prayer, for it was the practice of the righteous before you, and a means of nearness for you to Allah Almighty, an expiation for evil deeds, a deterrent from sin, and a repeller of disease from the body.""**"
"3521","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ was pleased with a man's conduct, he would command him to pray."
"3522","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded them to pray at night, whether little or much, and to make the last of that the Witr."
"3523","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they must never abandon night prayer, even for the duration of milking a sheep. > **""Do not ever abandon night prayer, even for the milking of a sheep.""**"
"3524","Jundub ibn Sufyan رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ loved Tahajjud in the night, whether half of it, a third of it, a quarter of it, the duration of milking a camel, or the duration of milking a sheep."
"3525","Iyas ibn Muawiyah al-Muzani reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that night prayer is indispensable even for the duration of milking a sheep, and that whatever is prayed after the Isha prayer counts as of the night. > **""Night prayer is indispensable, even for the milking of a sheep, and whatever is after the Isha prayer is of the night.""**"
"3526","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that when night standing was mentioned, one of those present informed them that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had said that it may be half the night, a third of it, a quarter of it, the duration of milking a camel, or the duration of milking a sheep. > **""Half of it, a third of it, a quarter of it, the duration of milking a camel, the duration of milking a sheep.""**"
"3527","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded night prayer and encouraged it, even saying that it is binding even if only one rakah, and that once the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to find a man performing supererogatory bowing after the congregational prayer had been established, whereupon he further asked whether they would not desist, asking whether two prayers could be together. > **""Upon you is night prayer, even if only one rakah. Will you not desist? Two prayers together?""**"
"3528","Ubaydah al-Mulayki رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he used to say that O People of the Quran, they must not take the Quran as a pillow, but recite it with its due recitation in the hours of night and day, remember what is in it that they may succeed, and not hasten its reward, for indeed it has its reward. > **""O People of the Quran, do not take the Quran as a pillow, but recite it with its due recitation in the hours of the night and day, and remember what is in it that you may succeed, and do not hasten its reward, for indeed it has its reward.""**"
"3529","Sahl ibn Sad رضي الله عنه reports that Jibril عليه السلام came to the Prophet ﷺ and said to him that he may live as long as he wishes but he shall die, do whatever he wishes but he shall be recompensed for it, love whomever he wishes but he shall part from him, and know that the honour of the believer lies in standing at night and his might lies in his independence of people. > **""O Muhammad, live as long as you wish, for you shall die, and do as you wish, for you shall be recompensed for it, and love whomever you wish, for you shall part from him, and know that the honour of the believer is his standing at night, and his might is his independence of people.""**"
"3530","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever among them prays at night should recite aloud, for the angels pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and the believing jinn in the air and his neighbours dwelling with him in his abode pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and that by his loud recitation he drives away from his house and the houses around it the corrupt among jinn and rebellious devils. He further explained that the house in which the Quran is recited is covered with a tent of light by which the inhabitants of heaven are guided, just as one is guided by a brilliant star in the depths of the seas and in barren land. When the companion of the Quran dies, that tent is raised, so the angels look from heaven and no longer see that light, and the angels receive him from heaven to heaven and invoke blessings upon his spirit among the spirits. Then the guardian angels who were with him come forth to meet him, and the angels seek forgiveness for him until the Day he is raised. No man learns the Book of Allah then prays an hour of the night except that the passing night enjoins the coming night to awaken him at his hour and to be light upon him. When he dies and his family are engaged in preparing him, the Quran comes in a beautiful comely form and stands at his head until he is wrapped in his shrouds, with the Quran upon his chest beneath the shroud. When he is placed in his grave, the earth levelled over him, and his companions disperse from him, Munkar and Nakir come to him and sit him up in his grave, whereupon the Quran comes until it stands between him and them. They say to it to stand aside that they may question him, but it replies by the Lord of the Kabah that he is indeed its companion and intimate, that it will never forsake him in any state, and that if they were commanded with anything they should proceed with what they were commanded and leave its place, for it will not part from him until it admits him into Paradise. Then the Quran looks to its companion and says that it is the Quran which he used to recite aloud and softly and love, that it is his beloved, and whomever it loves Allah loves, and that after the questioning of Munkar and Nakir there shall be no anxiety nor grief upon him. Munkar and Nakir question him and ascend, and he remains with the Quran, which promises to spread for him a thousand soft beds and cover him with a fine beautiful covering, because he kept his night wakeful and wearied his day. The narrator states that the Quran then ascends to heaven swifter than the blink of an eye and asks Allah for that for him, and He grants it, so a million of the nearest angels of the sixth heaven descend with it. The Quran comes and greets him and asks whether he felt lonely, informing him that since parting from him it only spoke to Allah Blessed and Exalted until a bed, covering, and key were prepared for him, and that it has brought them, so let him rise that the angels may spread his bedding. The angels gently raise him, then a space of four hundred years' journey is opened for him in his grave, then a bed is laid whose lining is of green silk stuffed with fragrant musk, with cushions of green brocade and thick silk at his feet and head, and two lamps of the light of Paradise are kindled for him at his head and feet, shining until the Day of Resurrection. Then the angels lay him upon his right side facing the Qiblah, then jasmine of Paradise is brought and raised above him, and he remains with the Quran. The Quran takes the jasmine and places it fresh upon his nose, so he inhales its fragrance until he is raised. The Quran returns to his family, informs them every day and night, watches over them as a compassionate father watches over his child with good, gives glad tidings if any of his children learns the Quran, and supplicates for righteousness and devotion if his descendants prove evil, or as he stated. > **""Whoever among you prays at night, let him recite aloud, for indeed the angels pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and indeed the believing jinn who are in the air and his neighbours with him in his dwelling pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and he drives away by his loud recitation from his house and from the houses around him the corrupt among jinn and the rebellious devils.""**"
"3531","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah does not disappoint a man who stands in the depth of the night and opens Surah al-Baqarah and Aal Imran. > **""Allah does not disappoint a man who stands in the depth of the night and opens Surah al-Baqarah and Aal Imran.""**"
"3532","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ that in Paradise there is a chamber whose outside is seen from its inside and whose inside is seen from its outside. Abu Malik al-Ashari رضي الله عنه asked for whom it is, O Messenger of Allah, and he replied that it is for whoever refines his speech, feeds food, and spends the night standing while people sleep. In the narration of Ahmad the questioner is named as Abu Musa al-Ashari رضي الله عنه. > **""Indeed in Paradise there is a chamber whose outside is seen from its inside and whose inside is seen from its outside. It is for whoever refines his speech, feeds food, and spends the night standing while people sleep.""**"
"3533","Abu Malik al-Ashari رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that in Paradise there are chambers whose inside is seen from their outside and whose outside is seen from their inside, which Allah has prepared for whoever feeds food, softens speech, fasts consecutively, and stands at night while people sleep. > **""Indeed in Paradise there are chambers whose inside is seen from their outside and whose outside is seen from their inside. Allah has prepared them for whoever feeds food, softens speech, fasts consecutively, and stands at night while people sleep.""**"
"3534","Abu Muaniq al-Ashari reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that in Paradise there are chambers whose outside is seen from their inside and whose inside is seen from their outside, which Allah Almighty has prepared for whoever feeds food, fasts perpetually, and prays at night while people sleep. > **""Indeed in Paradise there are chambers whose outside is seen from their inside and whose inside is seen from their outside. Allah Almighty has prepared them for whoever feeds food, fasts perpetually, and prays at night while people sleep.""**"
"3535","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever spends the night praying in lightness of food and drink will have the wide-eyed Houris encircling him until morning. > **""Whoever spends the night praying in lightness of food and drink, the wide-eyed Houris circle around him until morning.""**"
"3536","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that three are those whom Allah loves, smiles upon, and rejoices in: the one who, when a contingent is exposed, fights behind it with his own self for Allah Almighty, whether he is killed or Allah grants him victory and suffices him, so He says to behold how this servant of His endured with his own self for Him; the one who has a beautiful wife and a soft fine bed yet rises in the night, abandoning his desire and remembering Him, though had he wished he could have slept; and the one who is on a journey with a party who keep awake then slumber, so he rises in the last part of the night in hardship and ease in secret devotion. > **""Three are those whom Allah loves, smiles upon, and rejoices in: the one who, when a contingent is exposed, fights behind it with his own self for Allah Almighty, whether he is killed or Allah grants him victory and suffices him, and He says, Behold this servant of Mine, how he endured with his own self for Me; the one who has a beautiful wife and a soft fine bed, yet rises in the night, abandoning his desire and remembering Me, though had he wished he could have slept; and the one who is on a journey with a party who keep awake then slumber, so he rises in the last part of the night in hardship and ease in secret.""**"
"3537","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports in a Marfu narration that three are those whom Allah Almighty loves: a man who stands at night reciting the Book of Allah, a man who gives charity concealing it even from his left hand, and a man who is in a detachment whose companions flee, yet he advances to face the enemy. > **""Three are those whom Allah Almighty loves: a man who stands at night reciting the Book of Allah, a man who gives charity concealing it from his left hand, and a man who is in a detachment whose companions flee, yet he advances to face the enemy.""**"
"3538","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that their Lord wonders at two men, a man who springs from his bed and quilt among his family and beloved toward his prayer, whereupon their Lord says to His angels to behold His servant who sprang from his bed and quilt, from among his beloved and family, toward his prayer, desiring what is with Him and fearing what is with Him, then the narrator mentioned the like with an abridged mention of charity. > **""Our Lord wonders at two men: a man who springs from his bed and quilt among his family and beloved toward his prayer, so our Lord says, O My angels, behold My servant who sprang from his bed and quilt, from among his beloved and family, toward his prayer, desiring what is with Me and fearing what is with Me.""**"
"3539","In the narration of al-Tabarani in al-Kabir, the like is reported from him as Mawquf, except that it states that there is also a man whom none knows, who perfects wudu, invokes blessings upon Muhammad ﷺ, praises Allah, and begins recitation, so Allah smiles upon him and says to behold His servant whom none sees but He. > **""Behold My servant, whom none sees but Me.""**"
"3540","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that indeed Allah smiles upon two men: a man who rises on a cold night from his bed, quilt, and covering, performs wudu, then stands for prayer, whereupon Allah Almighty asks His angels what moved this servant of His to do what he did. They reply that it was hope for what is with Him and fear of what is with Him, whereupon He declares that He has granted him what he hoped and secured him from what he feared. > **""Indeed Allah smiles upon two men: a man who rises on a cold night from his bed, quilt, and covering, performs wudu, then stands for prayer. Allah Almighty says to His angels, What moved this servant of Mine to do what he did? They say, Our Lord, hope for what is with You and fear of what is with You. He says, Indeed I have granted him what he hoped, and secured him from what he feared.""**"
"3541","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that indeed Allah smiles upon three persons: a man who rises in the depth of the night, perfects purification, and prays; a man who sleeps while prostrating, and, as the narrator thought, one who is in a squadron that flees while he remains upon his steed, though had he wished to depart he could have departed. > **""Indeed Allah smiles upon three persons: a man who rises in the depth of the night, perfects purification, and prays; a man who sleeps while prostrating; and a man who is in a squadron that flees while he remains upon his steed, though had he wished to depart he could have departed.""**"
"3542","Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that envy is only in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, so he stands with it, abiding by it, declaring its lawful lawful and its unlawful unlawful; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth, so he joins thereby his kin and womb and acts in obedience to Allah. > **""Envy is only in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, so he stands with it, declaring its lawful lawful and its unlawful unlawful; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth, so he joins thereby his kin and acts in obedience to Allah.""**"
"3543","Samurah ibn Jundub رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to say to them that there is no envy in the world except in two cases: that a man envies one to whom Allah has given abundant wealth which he spends profusely, so the other says that had he wealth he would spend like this man spends and better, thus envying him; and that a man recites the Quran and stands at night with it while a man beside him knows not the Quran, so he envies him for his standing and for what Allah Almighty taught him of the Quran, saying that had Allah taught him the like he would stand as he stands. > **""There is no envy in the world except in two cases: a man to whom Allah gives abundant wealth which he spends profusely, so another says, Had I wealth I would spend as this man spends and better; and a man who recites the Quran and stands at night with it, so one beside him who knows not the Quran envies him for his standing and for what Allah Almighty taught him of the Quran, saying, Had Allah taught me the like I would stand as he stands.""**"
"3544","Yazid ibn al-Akhnas رضي الله عنه, who had companionship, reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there is no rivalry among them except in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, so he stands with it through the hours of night and day and follows what is in it, whereupon another says that had Allah given him what He gave so-and-so he would stand with it as he stands with it; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth which he spends and gives in charity, whereupon another says the like. > **""There is no rivalry among you except in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, so he stands with it through the hours of the night and day and follows what is in it, so a man says, Had Allah given me what He gave so-and-so I would stand with it as he stands with it; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth, so he spends and gives charity, so a man says the like.""**"
"3545","Abu Said رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there is no envy except in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran which he recites through the hours of night and day, so another says that had he been given the like of what this man was given he would do as he does; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth which he spends in its right, so another says that had he been given the like of what this man was given he would do as he does. > **""There is no envy except in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Quran, so he recites it through the hours of the night and day, and another says, Had I been given the like of what this man was given I would do as he does; and a man to whom Allah has given wealth, so he spends it in its right, and another says, Had I been given the like of what this man was given I would do as he does.""**"
"3546","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that a man of the Ansar came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه and Umar رضي الله عنه were with him, and mentioned that so-and-so had sown a crop which had multiplied, or as he said, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what that amounts to, and explained that two light rakahs are better for him than all of that, the world and what is upon it, and that had they done all they were commanded they would eat amply without wretchedness. > **""What is that? Two light rakahs are better for you than all of that, the world and what is upon it. Had you done all you are commanded, you would eat amply without wretchedness.""**"
"3547","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه would hardly ever fast, saying that when he fasted he grew weak for prayer, and prayer was more beloved to him than fasting, though when he did fast he fasted three days of the month."
"3548","Abu Ubaydah reports from his mother that she never saw Abdullah fasting at all except two days besides Ramadan, and the narrator said he does not know what was the matter of those two days."
"3549","Al-Shabi reports from his uncle that he frequented Ibn Masud for a year and never saw him fasting at all except in Ramadan, and that he would drink strong nabidh from green jars."
"3550","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that prayer is more beloved to him than fasting, and the narrator states that he would not pray Duha."
"3551","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that so long as one is in prayer one knocks at the door of the King, and whoever knocks frequently at the door of the King, it is soon opened for him. > **""So long as you are in prayer, you knock at the door of the King, and whoever knocks frequently at the door of the King, it is soon opened for him.""**"
"3552","From Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه it is also reported that the parable of one who persists in prayer is like one who knocks at a door, and whoever persists in knocking, it is soon opened for him. > **""The parable of one who persists in prayer is like one who knocks at a door, and whoever persists in knocking, it is soon opened for him.""**"
"3553","Al-Shabi said that Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه would not pray Duha, but would pray between Zuhr and Asr, along with a long portion of the night."
"3554","Ali ibn Abi Hamlah and al-Awzai said that Abdullah ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما would prostrate a thousand prostrations every day."
"3555","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said that so-and-so prays at night but steals when morning comes, whereupon he replied that what he says, meaning his prayer, will prevent him. > **""What he says will prevent him.""**"
"3556","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that a man said to the Prophet ﷺ that so-and-so prays but steals when morning comes, whereupon he replied that what he says, meaning his prayer, will soon prevent him. > **""What he says will soon prevent him.""**"
"3557","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that whoever is not forbidden by his prayer from indecency and wrongdoing only increases in distance from Allah. > **""Whoever is not forbidden by his prayer from indecency and wrongdoing only increases in distance from Allah.""**"
"3558","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that whoever is not commanded by his prayer to good nor forbidden by it from wrongdoing only increases in distance from Allah."
"3559","Waqid, freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that whoever obeys Allah Almighty has indeed remembered Him, even if his prayer, fasting, and recitation of the Quran are little, and whoever disobeys Allah has not remembered Him, even if his prayer, fasting, and recitation of the Quran are abundant. > **""Whoever obeys Allah Almighty has indeed remembered Him, even if his prayer, fasting, and recitation of the Quran are little, and whoever disobeys Allah has not remembered Him, even if his prayer, fasting, and recitation of the Quran are abundant.""**"
"3560","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that a freedwoman of the Prophet ﷺ would fast by day and stand at night, so it was mentioned to him that she fasts by day and stands at night, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ explained that every deed has its ardour and ardour lapses into lassitude, so whoever's lassitude tends toward his Sunnah is guided, and whoever's tends to other than that is astray. > **""Indeed for every deed there is ardour, and ardour lapses into lassitude, so whoever's lassitude is toward my Sunnah is guided, and whoever's lassitude is toward other than that is astray.""**"
"3561","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah does not tire until they tire. > **""Indeed Allah does not tire until you tire.""**"
"3562","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما and Aishah رضي الله عنها report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered the mosque to find a sound like the humming of bees from recitation of the Quran, whereupon he said that Islam shall indeed expand, then there shall be lassitude, so whoever's lassitude tends toward excess and innovation, those are the people of the Fire. > **""Indeed Islam shall expand, then there shall be lassitude, so whoever's lassitude is toward excess and innovation, those are the people of the Fire.""**"
"3563","Jadah ibn Hubayrah رضي الله عنه reports that a freedman of Banu Abd al-Muttalib was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ who prayed and never slept, and fasted and never broke fast, whereupon he said that he himself prays and sleeps, fasts and breaks fast, and that for every deed there is ardour and for every ardour there is lassitude, so whoever's lassitude is toward the Sunnah is guided, and whoever's is toward other than that is astray. > **""I pray and I sleep, and I fast and I break fast. For every deed there is ardour, and for every ardour there is lassitude, so whoever's lassitude is toward the Sunnah is guided, and whoever's is toward other than that is astray.""**"
"3564","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they should take of worship what they can bear, for Allah does not weary until they weary. > **""Take of worship what you can bear, for indeed Allah does not weary until you weary.""**"
"3565","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ that they must take of deeds what they can bear, for Allah does not tire until they tire. > **""Take of deeds what you can bear, for indeed Allah does not tire until you tire.""**"
"3566","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the soul grows weary, and none of them knows the extent of the term, so let one consider of worship what he can bear, then persist therein, for the most beloved deeds to Allah are those persisted in, even if little. > **""Indeed the soul grows weary, and none of you knows the extent of the term, so let one consider of worship what he can bear, then persist therein, for indeed the most beloved deeds to Allah are those persisted in, even if little.""**"
"3567","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that she saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ praying one night, so she stood behind him and prayed with his prayer. When he sat he lightened his standing and prayed two light rakahs, then saluted, then rose and prayed two rakahs, then saluted, making her hear the salutation. Then he turned to her and thrice told her to burden herself only with deeds within her capacity. > **""Burden yourself with of deeds what you can bear.""**"
"3568","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that some people who strove strenuously in worship were mentioned before the Prophet ﷺ, whereupon he said that it is the ardour of Islam and its zeal, and for every deed there is zeal, so whoever's lassitude tends toward moderation, how excellent it is, and whoever's lassitude tends toward sins, those are the destroyed. > **""That is the need of Islam and its zeal, and for every deed there is zeal, so whoever's lassitude is toward moderation, how excellent it is, and whoever's lassitude is toward sins, those are the destroyed.""**"
"3569","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out of the house of Uthman ibn Mazun رضي الله عنه and stood at the door, asking Kahilah why she appeared dishevelled and whether Uthman was not present. She replied that he was, but that he had not lain upon a bed for such and such a time and fasts the day without breaking fast. He told her to order him to come to him. When Uthman came and she informed him, he set out to the Prophet ﷺ, found him in the mosque, sat beside him, but the Prophet ﷺ turned away from him. He wept, then said that he knew something concerning him had reached him. He asked whether he is the one who fasts the day and stands the night with his side never touching a bed. Uthman replied that he had done that seeking good, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ reminded him of the shares of his eye, body, and wife, commanded moderation in fasting, sleeping, standing, and approaching his wife, citing his own practice of fasting and breaking fast, sleeping and praying, and approaching women, and explaining that whoever takes his Sunnah is guided and whoever abandons it is astray. He further explained that every deed has ardour and every ardour has lassitude, so lassitude toward heedlessness is ruin, while lassitude toward the obligatory harms not its companion, and enjoined taking of deeds what one can bear, for he was sent with the upright easy creed, so one must not burden oneself in the worship of one's Lord, for one knows not the length of one's life. > **""Your eye has a share, and your body has a share, and your wife has a share. So fast and break fast, sleep and stand, and approach your wife. For I fast and break fast, sleep and pray, and approach women. Whoever takes my Sunnah is guided, and whoever abandons it is astray. Take of deeds what you can bear, for I was sent with the upright easy creed, so do not burden yourself in the worship of your Lord, for you know not the length of your life.""**"
"3570","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that they must not contend with this night, for they will never bear it, and that when one of them grows drowsy he should depart to his bed, for that is safer for him."
"3571","Masruq said that when Abdullah rose they would remain sitting after him, with people firming their recitation, and when they rose they prayed, until news of that reached him. When they entered upon him he asked whether they burden people with what Allah Almighty has not burdened them, praying in such a manner that people deem it obligatory upon them, and told them that if they must do so, it should be in their houses."
"3572","Abu al-Tufayl Amir ibn Wathilah رضي الله عنه reports that a man passed by a company, greeted them, and they returned his greeting. When he had passed them, one of them said that he dislikes this man for Allah. The assembly rebuked him and ordered him to make it clear, sending one of them to inform him. Their messenger caught up with him and told him what was said, so the man turned back until he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and complained that he had passed a Muslim assembly containing so-and-so, greeted them, they returned his greeting, but then one of them informed him that so-and-so declared hatred for him for Allah, and he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to summon him and ask for what he hates him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ summoned him and questioned him about what the man had reported, and he confessed that he had indeed said it. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked why he hates him, he replied that he is his neighbour and knows him well, and that by Allah he never saw him pray any prayer except the prescribed prayer which the righteous and the wicked both pray. The other man interposed asking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to ask whether he ever saw him delay it beyond its time or perform its bowing and prostration poorly, and when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked him, he replied in the negative. He then said that by Allah he never saw him fast except the month which the righteous and the wicked both fast. The other man again asked that he be questioned whether he ever saw him negligent therein or detracting from its right, and he again replied in the negative. He then said that by Allah he never saw him give a beggar anything nor spend anything of his wealth in any path of good, except the charity which the righteous and the wicked both render. The other man again asked that he be questioned whether he ever concealed anything of Zakah or haggled with its collector, and he again replied in the negative, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dismissed the accuser, expressing that he does not know, perhaps the man is better than him. > **""Arise, I do not know, perhaps he is better than you.""**"
"3573","Wathilah ibn al-Asqa رضي الله عنه reports that a man of unsightly form, cross-eyed, short-necked, bandy-legged, dark, left-handed, spare in the shoulders, and wide between the knees came to the Prophet ﷺ and asked him to inform him of what Allah had made obligatory upon him. When he was informed, he pledged to Allah that he would add nothing beyond the obligation. The Prophet ﷺ asked why, and he replied that it is because his Lord created him thus afflicted. He then turned away, whereupon Jibril عليه السلام came and asked where the one displeased with his Lord is, declaring that he had reproached a Generous Lord, so He reproached him in return, and told him to say to him whether he would not be pleased to be raised on the Day of Resurrection in the form of Jibril. The Prophet ﷺ thus sent for the man and informed him that he had reproached a Generous Lord who had reproached him in return, asking whether he would not be pleased that Allah raise him on the Day of Resurrection in the form of Jibril. He replied affirmatively, O Messenger of Allah, then pledged to Allah that his body would bear whatever pleases Allah Almighty that it can bear. > **""You reproached a Generous Lord, so He reproached you in return. Would you not be pleased that Allah raises you on the Day of Resurrection in the form of Jibril?""**"
"3574","Jabir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that there is no male nor female but upon his head is tied a cord with three knots when he sleeps. If one of them awakes and remembers Allah Almighty a knot is loosened, and when he rises and performs wudu a knot is loosened, and when he rises for prayer all his knots are loosened. In the narration of Ahmad and Abu Yala there is the addition that he rises energetic, having attained good, but if he sleeps without remembering Allah until morning, his knots remain heavy upon him. In the narration of al-Tabarani in al-Awsat there is the addition that when he awakes Satan says to him that the night is long, so sleep, and Satan ties the cord upon him. > **""There is no male nor female but upon his head is tied a cord with three knots when he sleeps. If one of you awakes and remembers Allah Almighty a knot is loosened, and when he rises and performs wudu a knot is loosened, and when he rises for prayer all his knots are loosened.""**"
"3575","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه said that when one of you lies down to sleep, knots are tied over his head with a cord, and if he rises and remembers Allah Almighty one knot is loosened, and if he proceeds to perform ablution a second knot is loosened, and if he proceeds to pray a third knot is loosened, but if he reaches morning without having risen for anything in the night and without having prayed the dawn prayer, he reaches morning with it still upon him, meaning the cord."
"3576","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that a man who had slept through the night without praying until morning was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and Al-Hasan al-Basri remarked upon the heaviness of such urine. > **""Satan has urinated in his ear.""**"
"3577","Samurah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of Satan possessing a means of anointing and a means of licking, and of their effect upon remembrance and speech. > **""Satan has a kohl and a lick. When he anoints a man with his kohl, his eyes sleep away from remembrance, and when he gives him to lick from his lick, his tongue pours forth evil.""**"
"3578","Abdullah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described what comes to the servant who intends prayer in the night, and the contrasting calls of an angel and of Satan, and the differing morning states of obedience and disobedience. > **""When the servant intends prayer in the night, an angel comes to him and says to him: Rise, for morning has come, so pray and remember your Lord. Then Satan comes to him and says: The night is long upon you, and you will rise later. If he rises and prays, he reaches morning energetic, light in body and cool of eye, but if he obeys Satan until morning comes, Satan urinates in his ear.""**"
"3579","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to him and to Fatimah رضي الله عنها in the night and woke them for prayer, then returned to his house and prayed part of the night, and hearing no sound from them he returned and commanded them to rise, whereupon Ali answered while rubbing his eyes with an excuse concerning destiny, at which the Prophet ﷺ turned away striking his thigh and repeating his words and reciting a verse. > **""Rise and pray. You pray only what has been decreed for you, you pray only what has been decreed for you. And man is most contentious in disputation.""**"
"3580","Abu Malik al-Ashari رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the forgiveness granted to a man who rises in the night, wakes his wife, sprinkles water upon her face if sleep overcomes her, and joins her in remembrance of Allah Almighty for an hour of the night in their house. > **""There is no man who wakes in the night and wakes his wife, and if sleep overcomes her he sprinkles water upon her face, and they rise in their house and remember Allah Almighty for an hour of the night, except that they are both forgiven.""**"
"3581","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would not sleep except with the siwak beside him, and that whenever he awoke he began with the siwak."
"3582","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose in the night he would relieve himself, perform ablution and use the siwak, then send to seek perfume among the apartments of his wives."
"3583","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reports that sleep was mentioned in the presence of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who gave instruction concerning sleeping and what to do upon waking. > **""Sleep, and when you wake, clean your teeth with the siwak.""**"
"3584","Rabiah al-Jarshi reports that he asked Aishah رضي الله عنها what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would say when he rose in the night and with what he would begin, and she described his tenfold glorifications and his two tenfold supplications for forgiveness, guidance, provision and refuge from distress on the Day of Reckoning. > **""Allah is Greatest ten times, and praise be to Allah ten times, and glory be to Allah ten times, and there is no god but Allah ten times, and I seek Allah's forgiveness ten times. O Allah, forgive me and guide me and provide for me ten times. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from constriction on the Day of Reckoning ten times.""**"
"3585","Sad ibn Junadah رضي الله عنه reports that he was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Hanif and heard him speak of the forgiveness granted to one who rises in the night, performs ablution, rinses his mouth and repeats the appointed glorifications one hundred times each, excepting claims of blood and wealth which are not annulled. > **""Whoever rises in the night and performs ablution and rinses his mouth, then says glory be to Allah one hundred times and praise be to Allah one hundred times and there is no god but Allah one hundred times, his sins are forgiven, except for blood and wealth, for they are not annulled.""**"
"3586","Al-Numan ibn Bashir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed one who sleeps in the night intending to pray therein concerning placing and casting dust. > **""When one of you sleeps in the night intending to pray in the night, let him place a handful of dust at his right, and when he wakes let him throw pebbles to his left.""**"
"3587","Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak of two men from his Ummah, one of whom rises in the night and strives against himself toward purification while knots are upon him, and of the loosening of a knot with each act of ablution, and of what the Lord Almighty says from behind the veil concerning His servant and his request. > **""Two men from my Ummah: one of them rises in the night and strives with himself toward purification while knots are upon him. He performs ablution, and when he washes his hands a knot is loosened, and when he washes his face a knot is loosened, and when he wipes his head a knot is loosened, and when he washes his feet a knot is loosened. Then the Lord Almighty from behind the veil says: Look at this servant of Mine striving with himself. Whatever this servant of Mine asks of Me, it is his.""**"
"3588","Amr ibn Anbasah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning the form of the night prayer and the most answered supplication in the last depth of the night, together with an explanation of the word denoting answered supplication. > **""The night prayer is two by two, and the last depth of the night is the most answered in supplication.""**"
"3589","Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him with instruction concerning Witr before sleep and the form of the night prayer. > **""Perform Witr before you sleep, and the night prayer is two by two.""**"
"3590","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the form of the night prayer and the Witr at the end of the night. > **""The night prayer is two by two, and the Witr is one rakah at the end of the night.""**"
"3591","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ would pray in the daytime two by two."
"3592","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning a woman admitting anyone into her husband's house and rising from her bed for voluntary prayer except by his permission. > **""A woman shall not permit anyone into her husband's house except by his permission, nor shall she rise from her bed to pray voluntarily except by his permission.""**"
"3593","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose in the night and began his prayer with the takbir, he would glorify Allah with the appointed words of transcendence, repeat the declaration of unity three times, then seek refuge in Allah the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing from the accursed Satan, his pricking, his blowing and his spittle. > **""Glory be to You, O Allah, and with Your praise, and blessed is Your Name, and exalted is Your Grandeur, and there is no god besides You. There is no god but Allah, there is no god but Allah, there is no god but Allah. I seek refuge in Allah, the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing, from the accursed Satan, from his pricking and his blowing and his spittle.""**"
"3594","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ entered prayer in the night he would magnify, glorify and declare unity three times each, then seek refuge in Allah from the accursed Satan, his pricking, his blowing and his association. > **""Allah is Greatest three times, and glory be to Allah three times, and there is no god but Allah three times. O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the accursed Satan, from his pricking and his blowing and his association.""**"
"3595","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade a man to raise his voice with the Quran before the Isha prayer and after it, causing error to his companions while they were praying."
"3596","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed retreat in the last ten days, for whom a shelter of palm branches was built, and that on one night he put his head out and addressed the people concerning private converse with the Lord and not raising voices over one another. > **""O people, when the worshipper prays he converses privately with his Lord, blessed and exalted, so let him consider what he converses with Him, and let none of you raise his voice over another.""**"
"3597","Al-Bayadi رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came out to the people while they were praying with their voices raised in recitation, and instructed them concerning private converse with the Lord and not raising voices over one another with the Quran. > **""The worshipper converses privately with his Lord Almighty, so let him consider what he converses with Him, and let none of you raise his voice over another with the Quran.""**"
"3598","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that Abdullah ibn Hudhafah رضي الله عنه rose to pray and raised his voice in his prayer, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ addressed him by name and commanded him concerning whom to make hear. > **""O son of Hudhafah, do not make me hear, but make your Lord hear.""**"
"3599","Ali رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه would lower his voice in recitation, Umar رضي الله عنه would raise his voice in recitation, and Ammar رضي الله عنه would take from one surah and another, and that this was mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ, who questioned each of them and received their explanations, approving the soundness of Ammar's practice. > **""Why do you lower your voice? I make heard the One with Whom I converse privately. Why do you raise your voice in your recitation? I frighten Satan and awaken the drowsy. Why do you take from this surah and that surah? Do you hear me mixing into it what is not of it? No. Then all of it is good.""**"
"3600","Ammar ibn Yasir رضي الله عنه reports that Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه was asked why he lowered his voice in recitation and answered that he made heard the One with Whom he conversed, and Umar رضي الله عنه was asked why he raised his voice and answered that he awakened the drowsy, and another man was asked why he mingled his recitation and answered by asking whether he was heard adding what was not in it, and being told no, he replied that it was good to mingle one part with another."
"3601","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه and Aishah رضي الله عنها report from the Prophet ﷺ that he looked into a house while people were praying and raising their voices in recitation, and instructed them concerning private converse and consideration thereof. > **""The worshipper converses privately with his Lord, so let him consider what he converses with Him, and let none of you raise his voice over another with the Quran.""**"
"3602","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ compared the one who recites the Quran aloud to the one who gives charity openly, and the one who recites it softly to the one who gives charity secretly. > **""The one who recites the Quran aloud is like the one who gives charity openly, and the one who recites the Quran softly is like the one who gives charity secretly.""**"
"3603","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed whoever prays in the night to raise his voice in recitation, describing the angels praying with him and listening, the believing jinn in the air and as neighbours in his dwelling praying with him and listening, and the expulsion of wicked jinn and rebellious devils from his house and surrounding houses by his loud recitation. > **""Whoever among you prays in the night, let him raise his voice in his recitation, for the angels pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and the believing jinn who are in the air and his neighbours with him in his dwelling pray with his prayer and listen to his recitation, and by his raising his voice in his recitation he drives away from his house and from the houses around him the wicked among the jinn and the rebellious devils.""**"
"3604","Abu Bakrah رضي الله عنه reports that the recitation of the Prophet ﷺ was with prolongation, without warbling."
"3605","Alqamah ibn Qays reports that he spent a night with Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, who rose in the first part of the night and prayed reciting like the recitation of the imam in his neighbourhood mosque, with measured chanting and without warbling, making those around him hear without trilling his voice, until there remained of the darkness only as much as between the Maghrib call and the departure therefrom, when he prayed Witr."
"3606","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that whoever makes his own ears hear has not recited in a lowered voice."
"3607","Aishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the one who does not chant the Quran melodiously and his relation to us. > **""Whoever does not chant the Quran melodiously is not of us.""**"
"3608","Wathilah رضي الله عنه reports from the Prophet ﷺ concerning counting verses in voluntary prayer as distinct from obligatory prayer. > **""Count the verses in voluntary prayer, and do not count them in obligatory prayer.""**"
"3609","Tamim al-Dari رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the reward of standing a night in devotion recorded for one who recites a hundred verses. > **""Whoever recites one hundred verses, the standing of a night is recorded for him.""**"
"3610","Abu Hurairah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of one who prays in a night with a hundred verses not being recorded among the heedless, and of one who prays with two hundred verses being recorded among the God-fearing. > **""Whoever prays in a night with one hundred verses is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever prays with two hundred verses is recorded among the God-fearing.""**"
"3611","Fadalah ibn Ubayd رضي الله عنه and Tamim al-Dari رضي الله عنه report from the Prophet ﷺ concerning one who recites ten verses in a night, the surpassing worth of a qintar, and what the Lord Almighty will say on the Day of Resurrection concerning reciting and ascending by degrees and seizing Paradise and bliss. > **""Whoever recites ten verses in a night, a qintar is recorded for him, and a qintar is better than the world and what is in it. When the Day of Resurrection comes, your Lord Almighty will say: Recite and ascend with each verse a degree, until he reaches the last verse of what he knows. Your Lord Almighty will say to the servant: Take hold. The servant will say with his hand: O Lord, You know best. He will say: For this is eternity, and for this is bliss.""**"
"3612","Abu Umamah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ detailed the ranks recorded for reciting ten, one hundred, two hundred, four hundred, five hundred, six hundred, eight hundred, one thousand and two thousand verses in a night, and the measure and surpassing worth of a qintar. > **""Whoever recites ten verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites one hundred verses, the standing of a night is recorded for him, and whoever recites two hundred verses is recorded among the devout, and whoever recites four hundred verses is recorded among the worshippers, and whoever recites five hundred verses is recorded among the preservers, and whoever recites six hundred verses is recorded among the humble, and whoever recites eight hundred verses is recorded among the penitent, and whoever recites one thousand verses, a qintar accrues to him in the morning, and a qintar is one thousand two hundred ounces, better than what is between heaven and earth, or better than what the sun has risen upon, and whoever recites two thousand verses is of those made deserving.""**"
"3613","Ubadah ibn al-Samit رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ detailing the reward for reciting ten, one hundred, two hundred, four hundred, one thousand and two thousand verses in a night, and the measure of a qintar and its surpassing worth. > **""Whoever recites ten verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites one hundred verses, the standing of a night is recorded for him, and whoever recites two hundred verses is recorded among the devout, and whoever recites four hundred verses is recorded among the penitent, and whoever recites one thousand verses, a qintar accrues to him in the morning, one thousand two hundred ounces, an ounce better than what is between heaven and earth, and whoever recites two thousand verses is of those made deserving.""**"
"3614","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of reciting one hundred and two hundred verses at night, and of the qintar of reward for reciting from five hundred to one thousand verses, with a qirat thereof like a great mound. > **""Whoever recites one hundred verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites two hundred verses is recorded among the devout, and whoever recites from one thousand verses to five hundred verses, a qintar of reward is recorded for him, a qirat of the qintar like a great mound.""**"
"3615","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of the records for one who recites ten, one hundred and two hundred verses. > **""Whoever recites ten verses is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites one hundred verses is recorded among the devout, and whoever recites two hundred verses is recorded among the worshippers.""**"
"3616","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that whoever recites fifty verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless, and whoever recites one hundred verses is recorded among the devout, and whoever recites three hundred verses, a qintar is recorded for him, and whoever recites seven hundred prospers."
"3617","Said ibn al-Mundhir al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he should recite the Quran in three days, and the Prophet ﷺ assented, after which he continued to recite it thus until he died. > **""Yes, if you are able.""**"
"3618","Uthman ibn Amr ibn Aws reports from his father that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ among the delegation of Thaqif, whom the Prophet ﷺ would visit and converse with, until one night he was late, explaining that his daily portion of the Quran had come upon him and he disliked to interrupt it before completing it, and that in the morning they asked the companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ how they divided the Quran, who described portions of three, five, seven, nine, eleven and thirteen, and a final excellent portion from Qaf to the end of the Mufassal. > **""My portion of the Quran came upon me, and I disliked to interrupt it until I had completed it.""**"
"3619","Qays ibn Abi Sasah رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in how many days he should recite, and the Prophet ﷺ first appointed fifteen days then a week, while he insisted he found himself stronger, and that he continued thus for a time until age and weakening sight returned him to fifteen days, when he wished he had accepted the first concession of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. > **""In fifteen days. In a week.""**"
"3620","Muadh ibn Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ spoke of one who recites a thousand verses in the path of Allah being recorded on the Day of Resurrection with the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs and excellent companions. > **""Whoever recites one thousand verses in the path of Allah is recorded on the Day of Resurrection with the prophets and the truthful and the martyrs, and excellent are they as companions.""**"
"3621","Abu al-Ahwas reports that Abdullah رضي الله عنه said the Quran is not to be recited in less than three days, that it should be recited in seven, and that a man should guard his portion."
"3622","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه is reported to have recited the Quran in three days, taking little of it in the daytime."
"3623","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه said that whoever recites the Quran in less than three days is a reciter of rhymed verse."
"3624","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ spoke of the reward of reciting Say He is Allah, the One, Allah the Eternal. > **""Whoever recites: Say He is Allah, the One, Allah the Eternal, has recited a third of the Quran.""**"
"3625","Abdullah رضي الله عنه said that whoever recites three verses from Surah al-Baqarah has done abundant and excellent work."
"3626","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reports that a man brought his son to the Prophet ﷺ complaining that the boy recited from the mushaf by day and spent the night thus, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked what objection he had when his son reached morning in remembrance and spent the night in soundness. > **""What do you hold against your son reaching morning in remembrance and spending the night in soundness?""**"
"3627","Abdullah ibn Salam رضي الله عنه reports that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ whether he had recited the Quran, the Torah and the Gospel, and the Prophet ﷺ instructed him to distribute them across nights. > **""Recite this one night and this one night.""**"
"3628","Abu Masud al-Ansari رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said he did a deed secretly and it became manifest, and he rejoiced in it, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ informed him of a twofold reward for secrecy and openness. > **""Two rewards are recorded for you: the reward of secrecy and the reward of openness.""**"
